INSERT INTO `emp_profile` (`id`, `emp_id`, `brief_intro`, `academic`, `publication`, `comp_project`, `ong_project`) VALUES
(1, 2, 'Dr. Madhu Bala Singh, Scientist G, Joined ICMR, DMRC in 1984 & expertise in Micronutrient Deficiency Disorders & Nutrition & Growth. Mainly involved for last 37 years in research work pertaining to different aspects mainly MDDs, Malnutrition, Nutrition community Intervention and Dietary in desert and non desert parts of Rajasthan. Persuaded 32 research projects funded from various agencies of International and National repute viz., HarvestPlus, Washington, UNICEF; and DHR ICMR, DRDO, DFRL, MOHFW, AIIMS, etc. Awarded WHO Fellowship for undergoing training in ‘Food Safety and MDDs’ at Institute of Nutrition, Mahidol University, Bangkok, Thailand; Recognized as Ph.D. supervisor/ Reviewer for evaluation of Ph.D. thesis of foods & Nutrition from the MS University of Baroda, Vadodara, a WHO Collaborating Centre, JNV University, Jodhpur, Indore, MP and Delhi University; Nominated as Expert member of newly constituted Subject Expert Committee by DST-GOI on ‘Health, Food & Technology’ for evaluation of proposals received under Women Scientists Scheme-B, DST, New Delhi, Expert for evaluation of Research Proposal received from ‘Council of Science & Technology, UP, Vigyan Bhawan, Lucknow. Recognized as Core Member in Working Group 1 on ‘Operational & translation research to improve the contents and delivery of existing micronutrient supplementation or other programs based on existing knowledge’ under ‘Interagency group on Micronutrients’ constituted by Nutrition, ICMR, New Delhi. Awarded Fellowship of Indian Public Health Association. & Adjudged for first best paper award for oral presentation on ‘International Conference on Food and Nutrition Technology for Public Health Care’ at JNU, Delhi; Organized 14 conferences/ meetings/ Trainings and imparted Skill intensive trainings on CAB component & MDDs. Guiding M.Sc. students Dissertations (18). Published 58 research papers in International (Public Health Nutrition, Asian Pacific Journal of Clinical Nutrition, Journal of Arid Environment, Journal of Occupational Health) & National journals. Delivered 55 research papers in 76 scientific conferences/workshops (International-08 viz.I World Congress of Public Health Nutrition at Barcelona, ‘Pre congress workshop on Nutritional anemias’, Spain, Rome, Hangzau (China), Malaysia etc & National).', '-B.Sc from SGTB Khalsa College, Delhi university in Bio-Group: Botany (Honours)\r\n-M.Sc. from Miranda House, Delhi university in Anthropology-Medical (Physical): Specialization on Growth & Nutrition.\r\n-Ph.D. from Delhi University Anthropology- Medical (Physical): Specialization: Growth & Nutrition\r\n-Awarded WHO Fellowship for undergoing Training in ‘Food Safety & MDD’ at Institute of Nutrition, Mahidol University, Bangkok, Thailand for three Weeks from Mahidol University, Bangkok, Thailand\r\n-Undergone ‘CSDietary training’ at Mumbai in Pearl Millet project funded by Harvest Plus, Washington from HarvestPlus, Washington gave training at Mumbai\r\n-Undergone ‘Training of Trainers Course for Clinical, Anthropometric & Biochemical component of the Annual Health Survey’ held at NIHFW, New Delhi from 16th &17th September, 2010 fromNational -Institute of Family & Health Welfare, New Delhi\r\n-Training of Trainers Course for Clinical, Anthropometric & Biochemical component of Annual Health Survey from NIHFW, New Delhi\r\n-Implementation Course on ‘Laboratory Accreditation under ISO 17025:2005 Standards’ organized by Process Ethix, Delhi. from NFI, New Delhi\r\n-Training Programme on ‘Intellectual Property Rights (IPRs) and Related WTO Issues’ from CUTS Intl. with the support from DST, GOI at Jaipur\r\n-Taken Training for estimation of serum retinol for Vitamin A deficiency using HPLC Technique from Department of Biochemistry, Jamia Hamdard University, Delhi\r\n-Training on ‘ Assessing Feasibility of Establishing of NCD Info Base at Periphery Level’ from NCD, ICMR at DMRC, Jodhpur\r\n-New method of Micro-titer Plate Method (APDM) for estimation of Urinary Iodine Excretion (UIE) levels from Laboratory of Prof. M. G. Karmarkar, Regional Advisor, ICCIDD at AIIMS, Delhi Deputed', '1.	Bhattacharjee Debarati, Singh Madhu B. Assessment of Complementary Feeding Practices of Working and Non-Working Mothers and its Impact on Nutritional Status of Infants. IJARESM. 2021,9(1):1556-1560. .\r\n2.	Vyas Khushboo, Singh MB*, Pankaj Kumar, Aggarwal H. A Comparative Study of Depression (BDI) Among the Elderly Population Living in Community and Old Age Home in Desert Areas of Rajasthan. International Journal of Health Sciences & Research. 2019, 9(7): 37-42. IF. 0.667 .\r\n3.	Vyas Khushboo & Singh* Madhu. B. A study of Chronic Energy malnutrition among geriatric population residing in Old age Homes in desert areas of Rajasthan’. International Journal of Research and Analytical Reviews. 2019, 6(1): 148-150. .\r\n4.	Singh Madhu B. Food and Dietary culture of Arid lands of Rajasthan & its Nutrition Implications’ chapter in ‘Book on INDIAN FOOD ANTHROPOLOGY AND EAT RIGHT MOVEMENT’ edited by Nambiar Vanisha, published by Selective & Scientific books, New Delhi, 2021. ISBN:9788195149223. .\r\n5.	Singh MB, Premavalli KS. Impact of Supplementation of Pearl Millet (Pennisetum typhoides) products on Anemia, Malnutrition and Psychological attributes in school age children of Jodhpur, a desert district of Rajasthan, India. International Journal of Scientific Research. 2017, 6(12): 474-477. .\r\n6.	Singh MB, Parihar, N. Study of food and nutrient consumption pattern among children of 6-59 months of age with particular reference to Pearl Millet (Pennisetum typhoides) in Nagaur, a desert district of Rajasthan. International Journal of Food & Nutritional Sciences. 2017, 6(2): 13-18. .\r\n7.	Singh MB, Parihar, N. Food and nutrient consumption pattern in women of child bearing age with special emphasis on Pearl millet (Pennisetum typhoides) in Nagaur, a desert district of Rajasthan. Indian Journal of Applied Research. 2016, 6(10):15-17. .\r\n8.	Singh MB, Fotedar R, Chalga MS, Kumar P, Parihar, N. Zinc and other micronutrient deficiencies, under nutrition and morbidities in school children of desert area of Rajasthan. International Journal of Scientific Research. 2015, 4(6): 724-727. .\r\n9.	Singh MB, Lakshminarayana J, Parihar, N. Studies on the retention of Iron, Zinc, Phytate and Polyphenols content in different Raw varieties and Cooked recipes of Pearl Millet (Pennisetum typhoides) in Nagaur, a desert district of Rajasthan. International Journal of Food & Nutritional Sciences. 2015, 4(3): 56-62. .\r\n10.	Singh MB, Premavalli KS, Majumdar TK, Parihar N. Impact of consumption of certain electrolyte rich natural products on the Serum Na & K+ Profile, Anemia and Iodine Nutriture in desert population of Rajasthan. Journal Human Ecology, 2015, 50(2): 103-110. .\r\n11.	Singh MB, Fotedar R. Studies on the Nutritional status of school age children in Drought affected Desert environment of Western Rajasthan. Journal of Nutrition & Health sciences, 2015; 2(1): 102. .\r\n12.	Singh Madhu B, Lakshminarayana J. Breast feeding and weaning practices in thar desert of Rajasthan, India. Annals of Arid Zone, 2012,51(2): 109-113. .\r\n13.	Singh Madhu B, Sharma SK, Nair Siri, Pandey RM, Kapil Umesh, Singh C. Status of Iodine Content of Salt in four regions of India. Indian Journal of Pediatrics, 2011, 78 (6): 684-687. Impact Factor- 0.919 .\r\n14.	Singh Madhu B, Marwal Robin, Lakshminarayana J. Assessment of Iodine Deficiency Disorders in School age children in Jodhpur district of Rajasthan. Journal Human Ecology, 2010, 32 (2): 79-83. .\r\n15.	Singh Madhu B, Fotedar R, Lakshminarayana J. Micronutrient deficiency Status among women of desert areas of western Rajasthan. Pub. Health Nutrition, 2009, 12 (5): 624-629. .\r\n16.	Singh Madhu B, Lakshminarayana J, Fotedar R. Nutritional status of adult population of Raika community in Jodhpur, desert district, of Rajasthan. Journal Human Ecology, 2009, 26 (2): 77-80. .\r\n17.	Singh Madhu B, Lakshminarayana J, Fotedar R. Chronic energy deficiency and its association with dietary factors in Adults of drought affected desert areas of Western Rajasthan. Asia Pac J Clin Nutr. 2008, 17(4): 580-585. .\r\n18.	Singh Madhu B, Fotedar R, Lakshminarayana J. Camel Milk Consumption Pattern and Its Association with Diabetes among Raika Community of Jodhpur District of Rajasthan. Ethno-Med., 2008, 2 (2): 103-105. .\r\n19.	Singh Madhu B, Fotedar R, Lakshminarayana J, Anand PK. Studies on the nutritional status of under five children in drought affected desert area of Western Rajasthan. Pub. Health Nutrition, 2006, 9(8): 961-967. .\r\n20.	Singh Madhu B, Lakshminarayana J, Fotedar R, Anand PK. Childhood illnesses and malnutrition in under five children in drought affected desert area of western Rajasthan, India. Journal of Communicable Disease, 2006, 38 (1): 88-96. .\r\n21.	Singh Madhu B, Lakshminarayana J, Fotedar R. Studies on the nutritional status of rural population in desert area of Rajasthan. Proc. of National Symposium on Tribal Health (19-20th October, 2006 at Regional Medical Research Center For tribals, Jabalpur) Indian Council of Medical Research. 2007, 91-196. .\r\n22.	Singh Madhu B, Fotedar R, Lakshminarayana J. Occupational morbidities and their association with nutrition and environmental factors among textile workers of desert areas of Rajasthan. Jour. Occupational Health. 2005, 47 (5): 371–377. .\r\n23.	Singh Madhu B, Lakshminarayana J, Fotedar R. Smoking pattern among the workers engaged in Textile industries of desert districts of Rajasthan. Indian J Med. Sci., 2004, 58 (11): 443-445. .\r\n24.	Singh Madhu B. Anthropometric assessment of the nutrition in female children of Thar desert of Rajasthan. Annals of Arid Zone, 2004,43(2): 199-203. .\r\n25.	Singh Madhu B. Nutritional aspects of children residing in desert areas of Rajasthan. Proc. of Workshop on Research Methodologies for Micronutrients Research, (22-24th March, 2002 at Ranikhet, Uttaranchal), Indian Council of Medical Research, 2004, 21-25. .\r\n26.	Singh Madhu B, Haldiya KR, Lakshminarayana J. Morbidity Pattern and its association with malnutrition in preschool children in desert areas of Rajasthan. Journal of Arid Environments, 2002, 51: 461-468. .\r\n27.	Singh Madhu B, Lakshminarayana J. Study on prevalent Maternal Beliefs regarding diet during common childhood illnesses in Thar desert, Rajasthan, Annals of Arid Zone, 2002, 41(1): 85-88. .\r\n28.	Singh Madhu B, Haldiya KR, Lakshminarayana J. Food habits in semi-arid areas of Rajasthan. Jour. Human Ecology, Special Issue No. 7, 1999, 115-117. .\r\n29.	Singh Madhu B, Haldiya KR, Chaudhary, RC. Knowledge and attitude of pregnant women regarding AIDS in semi-arid area of Rajasthan. J. Commun. Dis., 1997, 29: 139-44. .\r\n30.	Singh Madhu B, Haldiya KR, Lakshminarayana J. Infant feeding and weaning practices in some rural areas of Rajas¬than. JIMA, 1997, 95: 576-78. .\r\n31.	Singh Madhu B, Haldiya KR, Yadav SP, Lakshminarayana J, Mathur ML, Sachdeva Raman, Beniwal VK. Nutritional status of school age children of salt-workers in Rajasthan. Indian J. Med. Res., 1996, 103: 304-309. .\r\n32.	Singh Madhu B, Singh Karam V. Studies on the role of demographic and socio-economic factors in the distribution of important vectors in a semi-arid area of Rajasthan. Proc. Intl. Sym. Vect. Vectorborne Dis. , 1995, 193-197. .\r\n33.	Singh Madhu B. Maternal beliefs and practices regarding the diet and use of herbal medicines during measles and diarrhoea in rural areas. Indian Pediatrics, 1994, 31: 340-343. .\r\n34.	Singh Madhu B, Lakshminarayana J, Haldiya KR. KAP study regarding common diseases among rural women inhabiting some semi-arid areas of Rajasthan. Annals of Arid Zone, 1993, 32: 113-4. .\r\n35.	Singh Madhu B. Dietary beliefs and practices of pregnant and lactating women in rural areas of Jaipur district (Raj.). Raj. Med. Jour., 1993. .\r\n36.	Singh Madhu B, Sagar AP. Assessment of nutritional status of adult population groups of Sikkim based on anthro¬pometry. Indian J. Prev. Soc. Med., 1993, 24: 51-55. .\r\n37.	Singh Madhu B,. Haldiya KR, Lakshminarayana J, Chand Ramesh. Social and demographic profile of selected villages in Jaipur district of Rajasthan. Raj. Med. Jour., 1993, 32: 103-108. .\r\n38.	Singh Madhu B*. Observations on the physical growth among bodh male children of Kulu Valley (H.P.). Indian J. Physical Anthropol. Human Genet. 1992; 18, 59-66. .\r\n39.	Singh Madhu B*, Haldiya KR, Lakshminarayana J, Chand Ramesh. Fertility and family planning practices in the vil¬lages of Jaipur district - A priliminary survey. Raj. Med. Jour., 1991, 30: 181-188. .\r\n40.	Singh, Madhu B*., Observations on the physiological and functional growth among bodh male children of Kulu valley (H.P.). Raj. Med. Jour., 1991, 30: 139-144. .\r\n41.	Fotedar R, Singh MB*, Chalga MS, Kumar P. A Study of Low Birth Weight, Morbidity, Mortality and their causes among neonates of Jodhpur, a desert district of Rajasthan. International Journal of Scientific Research. 2016, 5(4): 281-283. .\r\n42.	Fotedar R, Singh MB*, Chalga MS, Kumar P. Nutritional status along with morbidity and mortality of under five children- A Follow up study of registered infants up to 5 years in Jodhpur district of Rajasthan. Indian Journal of Applied Research. 2016,6(4):31-34. .\r\n43.	Fotedar R, Singh MB*, Chalga MS, Kumar P. A Follow up study of Nutritional status along with morbidity, mortality and immunization status among registered neonates up to 12 months in Jodhpur, a desert district of Rajasthan. International Journal of Scientific Research. 2016, 5(5): 393-396. .\r\n44.	Nair Siri, Singh Madhu B., Sharma SK, Pandey RM, Kapil Umesh. A Multicentric study on Validation of Spot Testing Kit. Indian Journal of Pediatrics, 2012; 79 (6): 751-754. .\r\n45.	. Lakshminarayana J, Haldiya KR, Singh Madhu B. Associated risk of morbidities of Tobacco smoking among opium consumers in rural areas of Rajasthan. Journal Human Ecology, 2011, 33(1): 35-39. .\r\n46.	Lakshminarayana J, Misra KN, Singh Madhu B, Kalundha RK. Opium consumption and its indirect effect on the fertility of women in desert villages of Rajasthan. Annals of Arid Zone, 2010, 49 (2): 121-125. .\r\n47.	Lakshminarayana J, Singh, Madhu B. Opium consumption among rural population in western Rajasthan: Some observations from the study. Journal Human Ecology, 2009, 25(1): 1-4. .\r\n48.	Fotedar R, Lakshminarayana J, Ramnath T, Singh Madhu, B. Nutritional status, infant feeding and supplementation pattern among the working women of Jodhpur city. Annals of Arid Zone, 2002, 41(2): 183-189. .\r\n49.	Haldiya KR, Bhandari N, Singh Madhu B, Beniwal VK, Lakshminarayana J. Chemical composition of Urinary calculi in desert region of Rajasthan, India. Jour. Human Ecology, 1999, 10: 69-72. .\r\n50.	Singh Karam V, Singh Madhu B. Studies on the role of socio-demographic factors in the distribution of rodents in a semi-arid area of Rajasthan. Annals of Arid Zone, 1996, 35: 69-72. .\r\n51.	Haldiya KR, Mathur ML, Sachdeva Raman, Beniwal VK, Yadav SP, Singh Madhu B Lakshminarayana J. Morbidi¬ty profile of desert population engaged in salt production in Rajasthan. JIMA, 1995, 93: 95-97. .\r\n52.	Haldiya KR, Singh Madhu B, Lakshminarayana J, Chand Ramesh. Assessment of the nutritional and immunization status of pre-school children in rural areas of Jaipur district, Rajasthan. Raj. Med. Jour., 1993, 32:37-44. .\r\n53.	Dam PK, Mahapatra A, Ganguly KK, Dixit AK, Lakshminarayana J, Singh Madhu B*, Sapra GP, Krishnama¬chari KAVR. Socio-demographic profile of tribal population in three districts of Rajasthan. The Eastern Anthropologist, 1993,45: 241-253. .\r\n54.	Bhasin MK, Shil AP, Singh Madhu B*, Walter H, Heidi Danker-Hopfe, Singh IP, Bhasin V, Bhardwaj Veena, Sudhakar K, Wadhavan Devala. 1987. Biology of People of Sikkim, India. Colour blindness, ear lobe attachment, mid phalangeal hair and behavioural traits. Anthrop. Anz., 1987, 45: 351-360. .\r\n55.	Bhasin MK, Walter H,. Chahal SMS, Bhardwaj Veena, Sudhakar K, Heidi Danker-Hopfe, Dannewitz Angela, Singh IP, Bhasin V, Shil Anu P, Singh Madhu B*, Wadhavan Devala. Studies on the variability of genetic markers. Z.Morph. Anthrop, 1986, 77: 49-86. .\r\n56.	Lakshminarayana J, Singh Madhu B, Dangi R, Soni, S. Meta Analysis of Micronutrient Deficiency Disorders and their Mapping in Rajasthan, International Journal of Environmental and Allied Sciences, .\r\n57.	Lakshminarayana J, Singh Madhu B. Estimating and adjusting for publication bias in vitamin A (VAD) in Meta analysis International Journal of Environmental and Allied Sciences, .\r\n58.	Lakshminarayana, J., Mishra, A., Singhvi, L. and Singh, Madhu B. Opium use And socio cultural factors associated in consumption in rural areas of Desert Rajasthan, India. International Journal of Environmental and allied Sciences.', '1.	Study of food and nutrient consumption pattern in women of child bearing age and 6-59 months of age children, with particular reference to Pearl millet consumption pattern and effects of storage, processing, & cooking practices on retention of Iron, Zinc, Phytate and Polyphenols in Nagaur, a desert district of Rajasthan. 2010 & 12. As PI, Extramural funding by HarvestPlus, Washington.\r\n2.	Development of IEC modules for the promotion of three local pearl millet preparations to improve the knowledge w.r.t. Pearl Millet consumption among the rural population of Nagaur district of Rajasthan- 2015-18. As PI, Extramural funding by Translational Research Cell, ICMR, Delhi\r\n3.	Assessment of Iodine deficiency disorder, Anemia and Nutrition Intervention in school age children of Jodhpur district of Rajasthan- 2010 & 2011, Collaboration with DFRL, Mysore. As PI, Extramural funding by DRDO, Min of Defence, GOI, DFRL, Mysore\r\n4.	Determination of Micronutrient Deficiency Disorders among Pregnant women and their association with the outcome of the pregnancy with respect to LBW babies - A hospital based study - 2013-16. As Guide, Extramural funding by Research Associate Fellowship, ICMR, Delhi.\r\n5.	Impact assessment of consumption of two electrolyte products on mineral profile & general health & nutrition profile of Adult population in desert areas of Rajasthan- 2008-09. Collaboration with DFRL, Mysore. As PI, Extramural funding by DRDO, Min of Defence, GOI, DFRL, Mysore\r\n6.	Validity of Spot Testing Kit in the Assessment of Iodine Content of Salt-A Multicentric Study- 2008-09. As PI, DMRC, Extramural funding by AIIMS, Delhi\r\n7.	Annual Health Survey- Clinical Anthropometric and Biochemical Component (CAB) Survey- 2013-2014. As Co-PI. Extramural funding by MOHFW, Delhi.\r\n8.	Sub-clinical vitamin-A deficiency among children (6-71 months) of Assam and Rajasthan – 1995-96. As Co-PI. Extramural funding by UNICEF.\r\n9.	Multi Indicator survey of Six desert districts of Rajasthan 1995-96. As Co-PI. Extramural funding by UNICEF.\r\n10	Multi Indicator survey of rural areas of ten districts of Rajasthan 1999. As Co-PI. Extramural funding by UNICEF.\r\n11.	Establishment of Health Care Delivery system for salt labourers under a systematic master plan be way of a comprehensive health survey of all salt labourers - 1993-96. As Co-PI. Funding by Salt Commissioner, Govt., of India, Jaipur\r\n12.	Nutritional status of elderly rural population & development of appropriate intervention model using existing health - 2013-17. As PI. In collaboration with State Health Department, Dr. SN Medical College, Jodhpur & CNRT Laboratory, ICMR, Delhi.\r\n13.	Estimation of Zinc deficiency, under nutrition and morbidities especially ARI, Diarrhoea and Fever in school children of Rajasthan- 2012-14. As PI.\r\n14.	Nutritional status along with morbidity & mortality of under five children- A Follow up study of earlier registered infants up to 5 years in Jodhpur’- 2010-15. As Co-PI.\r\n15.	Nutritional status along with morbidity and mortality of Neonates & infants in Jodhpur District of Rajasthan – Community Based Longitudinal Study - 2007-10. As Co-PI.\r\n16.	Meta Analysis of Intervention studies on Micronutrient Deficiency Disorders and their Mapping in Western Rajasthan, India-2006-07. As Co-PI.\r\n17.	Micronutrient deficiency disorders along with Nutritional Status & morbidity in pregnant & lactating women in desert areas of Rajasthan -2004-2007. As PI.\r\n18.	Nutrition Monitoring Survey on NNMB pattern in Jodhpur district of Rajasthan - 2005 – 2014. As PI.\r\n19.	Assessment of Nutritional status of rural population of Drought affected areas of Jodhpur district of Western Rajasthan - 2003-04. As PI.\r\n20.	Morbidity pattern and Nutrition among the workers engaged in Textile industry of Pali and Jodhpur districts of Rajasthan - 2001- 04. As PI.\r\n21.	Epidemiological study of various factors related urolithiasis in desert and non desert districts. 2001-2003. As Co-PI.\r\n22.	A preliminary survey of Nutritional status of adult population of Raika community in Jodhpur district, Rajasthan – 2003. As PI.\r\n23.	Nutritional status of the school age children in desert districts of Rajasthan - 1993-96. As PI.\r\n24.	Nutritional status of the pre school children in desert districts of Rajasthan - 1993-96. As PI.\r\n25.	Infant feeding &Weaning practices and Maternal beliefs regarding diet during common childhood illness in desert areas of Rajasthan -1993-96. As PI.\r\n26.	AIDS awareness among pregnant women in Jaipur city-1994-96. As PI.\r\n27.	Nutrition and health survey among Salt survey in Rajasthan - 1992-93. As Co-PI, Institutional.\r\n28.	A study of Nutrition and Health Status of the Tribal Population of three districts in Rajasthan in Collaboration with State Govt. - 1989-90. As Co-PI, Institutional.\r\n29.	Health & Nutrition survey of drought affected parts of Rajasthan 1987. As Co-PI, Institutional.\r\n30.	Base line health survey in the three districts of Rajasthan- 1986-87. As Co-PI, Institutional', '1.	Assessment of Iodine among pregnant and lactating women and 6-11 years children in selected districts of Rajasthan’. 2021-2022. As PI, Extramural funding by ICMR, Delhi.\r\n2.	Assessment of Iodine adequacy in Urban Slum Population of Jodhpur city. 2019-2022. As Guide, Extramural funding by Senior Research Fellowship ICMR, Delhi\r\n3.	Community based Nutrition Intervention models using Local food preparations of Pearl Millet & food habits to address the problem of anemia & malnutrition in Rajasthan. (Implementation of Leads of Translation Project on Pearl Millet), As PI, 2021-2022.'),
(2, 3, 'Joined ICMR – National Institute of Implementation Research, Jodhpur, Rajasthan (formerly Desert Medicine Research Centre) in year 2002. Have experience of community based epidemiological studies from the subjects of Nutrition, Occupational health problems, diabetes, malaria, cardiovascular diseases and tuberculosis. Had experience of investigating typhoid outbreak, hospital studies in tuberculosis and coronary artery diseases.', '- M.B.B.S. (2000) from Sarojini Naidu Medical College, Agra – Recognized by Medical Council of India, New Delhi.\r\n- M.A.E. (Master of Applied Epidemiology – 2009) from ICMR - National Institute of Epidemiology, Chennai, in affiliation with Sree Chitra Thirunal Institute for Medical Sciences & Technology, Thiruvananthapuram – Degree is recognized by Medical Council of India, New Delhi', '(1) Mohanty S.S., Purohit A., Anand P.K., Huda R.K., Sharma A.K. Burden of Sickle cell disease in tribal students in Maa-Baadi institutions in Southern Rajasthan - A pilot study. Indian J of Med Res 156 (2); Aug 2022: pp 269-274. \r\n(2) Dhikav V, Kumar P, Anand PK. Cardiovascular Comorbidities and Cognitive Impairment. OBM Geriatrics 2022; 6(4): 215; doi:10.21926/obm.geriatr.2204214.\r\n(3) Thomas BE, Thiruvengadam K, Raghavi S, Rani S, Vetrivel S , Rao VG, Yadav R, Bhat J , Vijayachari P, Purthy AJ, Hussain T, Krishna AKI, Joseph A, Bansal AK, Anand PK, Das P, John KR, Devi R K, Sunish P, Moral R, Azhagendran S, Chandrasekaran V, Toteja GS, Das M, Khan A. M., Kaur H. Understanding health care-seeking behaviour of the tribal population in India among those with presumptive TB symptoms. PLOS One 2021; 16 (5): pp 1-15.\r\n(4) Thomas BE, Thiruvengadam K, Chandrasekaran V, Srividya A, V. G. Rao, Vijayachari P, Yadav R, Raghavi V, Bansal AV, Krishna AKI, Joseph A,Purty AJ, Hussain T, Anand PK, Das P, John KR, Rekha Devi K.,  Sunish P, Azhagendran S, Azger D, Bhat J, Chadha VK, Toteja G. S., Raghunath D, Das M, Khan A.M., Kaur H. Prevalence of pulmonary tuberculosis among the tribal populations in India. PLOS One 2021; 16(6): e0251519.\r\n(5) Anand P.K; M. Singh; C.R. Meena; S. Lata; A. Jangid; M.R. Devanda; M.L. Mathur; M. Rathore; B.M. Singh; & P. Kumar. Study of active screening to detect un-registered open cases of tuberculosis in rural areas of capital district of Rajasthan, India. Journal of Tuberculosis Research 2021; 9: 276 – 283.\r\n(6) Singh M; Dhingra B; Bishnu B; Pandey D; Anand P.K.; Gupta S; Das V.R.; Kodan P; Kabra S.K. Assessment of prevailing practices for identification of tuberculosis in children admitted in nutritional rehabilitation centres: A multi centre study. Ind J Med Res 2021; 154.\r\n(7) Dusthackeer A, Kumar A, Mohanvel SK, B. Mahizhaveni, S. Shivakumar, S.Raghavi, S. Azhagendran, S. Vetrivel, Rao VG, Yadav R, Vijayachari P, Purthy AJ, Hussain T, Kashyap V, K. Rekha Devi, Krishnan AKI, Anand PK, Das P, Bansal AK, Das MC, Kaur H, D. Raghunath, Mondal R & Thomas BE. Mycobacterium tuberculosis strain lineage in mixed tribal population across India and Andaman Nicobar Island. World J Microbiol Biotechnol 2021; 37: 192\r\n(8) Dhikav V; Jadeja B; Anand P.K. Cardiovascular risk factors among older adults with cognitive impairment in primary care. International Psychogeriatrics 2021;\r\n(9) Manoj V Murhekar, Tarun Bhatnagar, Jeromie Wesley Vivian Thangaraj, V Saravanakumar, Muthusamy Santhosh Kumar, Sriram Selvaraju, Kirankumar Rade, CP Kumar, R Sabarinathan, Alka Turuk, Nivethitha N Krishnan, Aby Robinson, Nivetha Srinivasan, Smita Asthana, Rakesh Balachandar, Sampada Dipak Bangar, Avi Kumar Bansal, Jyothi Bhat, Vishal Chopra, Dasarathi Das, Alok Kumar Deb, Kangjam Rekha Devi, Gaurav Raj Dwivedi, S Khan, M Sunil Kumar, Avula Laxmaiah, Major Madhukar, Amarendra Mahapatra, Chethana Rangaraju, Jyotirmayee Turuk, Suresh Yadav, PK Anand, Rushikesh Andhalkar, Nimmathota Arlappa, Khalid Bashir, Dinesh Kumar Baradwaj, Pravin Bharti, Debdutta Bhattacharya, Sthita Pragnya Behera, Ashrafjit S Chahal, Debjit Chakraborty, Anshuman Chaudhury, Hirawati Deval, Sarang Dhatrak, Vikas Dhikav, Rakesh Dayal, Prathiksha Giridharan, Inaamul Haq, Babu Jagjeevan, Agam Jain, Arshad Kalliath, Srikanta Kanungo, T Karunakaran, Jaya Singh Kshatri, Niraj Kumar, Vijay Kumar, VG Kumar, Gangeti Gandhi Jayanthi Naga Lakshmi, Ganesh Mehta, Anindya Mitra, K Nagbhushanam, AR Nirmala, Subrat Kumar Palo, Ashok Kumar Pandey, Ganta Venkata Prasad, Uday Kumar Pucha, Mariya Amin Qurieshi, Vikas Sabaharwal, Seema Sahay, Ramesh Kumar Sangwan, Rochak Saxena, Krithikaa Sekar, Vijay Kumar Shukla, Hari Bhan Singh, Prashant Kumar Singh, Pushpendra Singh, Rajeev Singh, Mahendra Thakor, Dantuluri Sheethal Varma, Ankit Viramgami, Pradeep A Menon, Rajiv Yadav, Surabhi Yadav, Manjula Singh, Amit Chakrabarti, Aparup Das, Shanta Dutta, Rajni Kant, AM Khan, Kanwar Narain, Somashekar Narasimhaiah, Chandrasekaran Padmapriyadarshini, Krishna Pandey, Sanghamitra Pati, Hemalatha Rajkumar, T Ramesh, Arun Kumar Sharma, YK Sharma, Shalini Singh, Samiran Panda, DCS Reddy, Balram Bhargava. Prevalence of IgG antibodies against SARS-CoV-2 among the general population and healthcare workers in India, June–July 2021. Preprint with The Lancet; August 2021 \r\n(10) Murhekar MV, Bhatnagar T, Selvaraju S, Rade K, Saravanakumar V, Thangaraj JWV, Kumar MS, Shah N, Sabarinathan R, Turuk A, Anand PK, Asthana S, Balachandar R, Bangar SD, Bansal AK, Bhat J, Chakraborty D, Rangaraju C, Chopra V, Das D, Deb AK, Devi KR, Dwivedi GR, Khan SMS, Haq I, Kumar MS, Laxmaiah A, Mahapatra A, Mitra A, Nirmala AR, Pagdhune A, Qurieshi MA, Ramarao T, Sahay S, Sharma YK, Shrinivasa MB, Shukla VK, Singh PK, Viramgami A, Wilson VC, Yadav R, Kumar CPG, Luke HE, Ranganathan UD, Babu S, Sekar K, Yadav PD, Sapkal GN, Das A, Das P, Dutta S, Hemalatha R, Kumar A, Narain K, Narasimhaiah S, Panda S, Pati S, Patil S, Sarkar K, Singh S, Kant R, Tripathy S, Toteja GS, Babu GR, Kant S, Muliyil JP, Pandey RM, Sarkar S, Singh SK, Zodpey S, Gangakhedkar RR, Reddy DCS, Bhargava B. Prevalence of SARS-CoV-2 infection in India: Findings from the national serosurvey, May-June 2020. Indian J Med Res 2020; 152 (1): 48 – 60.\r\n(11) Anand PK. Developing “Community TB Health” a statistical model for ranking of districts TB programme performance in Rajasthan, India. International Journal of Scientific Research 2019; 8 (6): 1-3.\r\n(12) Mandal N, Anand PK, Gautam S, Hussain SDT. Diagnosis and treatment of paediatric tuberculosis: An insight review. Critical Review in Microbiology 2017; 43 (4), 466-480.\r\n(13) Anand PK. Indicators correlated with the performance of Revised National Tuberculosis Control Programme in Rajasthan, India, 2011-2014. World Journal of Pharmaceutical & Medical Research 2017; 3 (8): 303 – 311.\r\n(14) Anand PK. Does co-suffering by ischemic heart disease and tuberculosis exist in community endemic for these diseases? International Journal of Tropical Disease & Health 2015; 5: 1-10.\r\n(15) Anand PK, Dixit A K, Chauhan M K, Lata S. Magnitude, Distribution and Associated Socio-behavioral Risk Factors of Coronary Artery Disease: A Hospital Based Study from Desert Ecology in India. J Hum Ecol, 2013; 44 (3): 275-280.\r\n(16) Anand PK. Book: Malaria associated factors in Thar desert of Rajasthan, India. The Personal, household and environment related factors. Lambert Academic Publishing, Germany, 2011.\r\n(17) Anand PK, Swarn L, Yadav SP, & Singh H. Disease dynamics, distribution and surveillance of malaria in arid ecology of Jodhpur, Rajasthan, India during 2002-2006. Journal of Public Health & Epidemiology, 2011; 3: 301-307. \r\n(18) Dixit AK & Anand PK. A study of household disease burden and its associated factors in rural population of a desert district in India. Southern African J Epidemiology & Infection 2011; 26: 165 – 68.\r\n(19) Yadav SP, Anand PK, & Singh H. Awareness and practices about silicosis among the sandstone quarry workers in desert ecology of Jodhpur, Rajasthan, India. J Hum Ecol 2011; 33: 191 – 196.\r\n(20) Anand PK, Ramakrishnan R. Investigation of the outbreak of typhoid in a village of Thar desert, Rajasthan, India. Indian J Med Res 2010; 131: 799 – 803.\r\n(21) Anand PK, & Ramakrishnan R. Malaria associated factors in Thar desert of Rajasthan, India: A case-control study. J Rural Trop Public Health 2010; 9: 61 – 66.\r\n(22) Yadav SP, Singh H, Anand PK. Changing scenario of malaria in the Thar desert, India. Proceedings: National seminar on impact of climate change on biodiversity and challenges in Thar desert, organized by Desert Regional Centre (old name ZSI) Jodhpur, on 9th July 2010: pp 351 – 61.\r\n (23) Anand PK, Dixit AK, Sharma RC. Correlation of viral hepatitis and Chronic liver diseases and cirrhosis with malignant neoplasm of liver: a study in Rajasthan from public health perspective. Oikoassay 2006; 19 (1 & 2): 39 – 41. \r\n(24) Singh M.B., Fotedar R, Lakshminarayana J & Anand PK. Studies on the nutritional status of under five children in draught affected desert area of Western Rajasthan, India. Pub Health Nutrition 2006; 9: 961 – 67.\r\n(25) Singh M.B., Lakshminarayana J., Fotedar R & Anand PK. Childhood illnesses and malnutrition in under five children in draught affected desert area of Western Rajasthan, India. J Commun Dis 2006; 38 (1): 88 – 96.\r\n(26) Dixit AK, Anand PK, Sharma RC. A study of district level development factors influencing infant mortality rate and life expectancy in the Indian Thar Desert. Journal of Rural and Tropical Public Health 2006; 5: 42-45.\r\n', '• As Principal Investigator:\r\n1.	Factors associated with malaria in block Banar of Jodhpur district, Rajasthan – P.K. Anand & R. Ramakrishnan (AR 2008-09)\r\n2.	Clinical presentation, treatment outcome and risk factors of coronary artery disease among patients admitted in tertiary care hospital in Jodhpur – P.K. Anand (AR 2010-11)\r\n3.	Estimate the burden of TB among the tribal population and develop an innovative health system model to strengthen TB control in tribal areas, Rajasthan – P.K. Anand (AR 2018-20)\r\n4.	Systematic pulmonary tuberculosis case finding among malnourished children admitted in Nutritional Rehabilitation Centres, Rajasthan – PK Anand (AR 2018 – 21)\r\n• As Co-Investigator:\r\n1.	Assessment of nutritional status of rural population of draught affected areas of Jodhpur district of western Rajasthan (AR 2003-04)\r\n2.	Longitudinal studies for disease dynamics in desert ecosystem of Rajasthan: Study of socio-cultural, socio-economic and epidemiological aspects of malaria using Geographical Information System (AR 2004-05)\r\n3.	A study of the potential interventional variables associated with delay in diagnosis/treatment of pulmonary tuberculosis cases in the Thar desert of Rajasthan (AR 2004-05)\r\n4.	Retrospective analysis and prospective prioritization of disease burden in Rajasthan (AR 2004-05)\r\n5.	Household distribution of disease burden in community health management – A study in desert (AR 2004-05)\r\n6.	Assessment of impact of environmental pollution on human health in the city of Jodhpur, Rajasthan (AR 2004-05)\r\n7.	Confirmation of “Introduction, Transmission & Aggravation” conceptuality of desert malaria: Verification of research undertaken and software developed (AR 2005-06)\r\n8.	Occurrence and burden of disease across the districts of Rajasthan: A study of the year 2000 (AR 2005-06)', 'As Principal Investigator\r\n(1) A National Model to Measure Burden and Map Quality of Care for Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus in India, involving Medical Colleges through Primary Health Care Setup- A Feasibility Study (DNMS Study)\r\n(2) Active screening & motivating the persons with TB suggestive symptoms by trained youth club members of Nehru Yuva Kendra Sangathan (NYKS)\r\n(3) A feasibility study for implementation of innovative RBSK - NTEP collaboration module for active case finding of tuberculosis among school students: Multicentric study\r\n(4) To study the effectiveness of a tailored implementation strategy to improve adherence to a Tuberculosis Preventive Treatment\r\n(5) Strategic multimodal intervention in at-risk elderly Indians for prevention of dementia (SMRUTHI INDIA): A cohort multiple randomized controlled trial (cmRCT)\r\n(6) Model Rural Health Research Unit, Bhanpur Kalan, Jaipur, Rajasthan'),
(3, 4, 'Entomology- Vector biology and ecology: Surveillance of vector borne diseases; determination of Insecticide Resistance Status; Development & Evaluation of newer vector surveillance & control tools/compounds including their different molecular aspects', 'M.Sc., PhD ', '1.Rina Kumawat, Singh Karam V, Bansal SK, Singh Himmat. 2014. Use of different coloured ovitraps in the surveillance \r\nof Aedes mosquitoes in an arid-urban area of Western Rajasthan, India. Journal of Vector Borne Diseases,51: 320-326. \r\nIF: 1.19 \r\n2.Rina Kumawat, Singh Karam V, Bansal SK, Singh Himmat. 2016. Breeding places of Aedes aegypti, the vector of \r\ndengue/DHF in urban arid situations of western Rajasthan, India. Indian Journal of Applied Research, 6:546-548. \r\nIF: 3.91 \r\n3.Rina Kumawat, Singh Karam V. 2016. Effect of Rainfall on the Breeding of Aedes aegypti, A vector of Dengue/DHF \r\nand Chikungunya with respect to Container Positivity in Western Rajasthan. International Journal of Scientific \r\nResearch, 5: 594-596.IF: 3.51 \r\n4.Rina Kumawat, Lalthazuali, Sunita Patel, Rajesh Sharma, Amarpal Singh Bhadauriya, Sweta Bhan, Pankaj Ramteke, \r\nT.G. Thomas, Sujeet Kumar Singh. 2019. Entomological Survey during Zika Virus Infection in Ahmedabad, Gujarat. \r\nJournal of Communicable Diseases, 51(1):12-17.IF: 0.07 \r\n5.SN Sharma, Rina Kumawat, Sujeet Kumar Singh. 2019. Kyasanur Forest Disease: Vector Surveillance and its Control. \r\nJournal of Communicable Diseases, 51(2): 1-7.IF: 0.07 \r\n6.SN Sharma, Ram Singh, Rina Kumawat, Sujeet Kumar Singh. 2019. Scrub Typhus: Vector Surveillance and its Control. \r\nJournal of Communicable Diseases, 51(3): 55-61.IF: 0.07 \r\n7.SN Sharma, Ram Singh, Rina Kumawat, Sujeet Kumar Singh. 2019. Rodents as Public Health pests: Identification, \r\nSurveillance & its Management. Journal of Communicable Diseases, 51(4): 47-57.IF: 0.07 \r\n8. Amarpal Singh Bhadauriya, Pankaj U. Ramteke, Abhay Kumar Sharma, R.K. Meena, Rina Kumawat, Sweta Bhan, \r\nLalthazuali, Ram Singh and Arun Chauhan. 2020. Entomological Investigations of an Outbreak of Japanese Encephalitis \r\nin three districts of Chhattisgarh, India in the year 2017. International Journal of Advanced Research, 8(2):161-171 \r\nIF: 7.08 \r\n9.Lalthazuali, Abhay Kumar Sharma, TG Thomas, Sweta Bhan, Amarpal Singh Bhadauriya, Pankaj U Ramteke, Rina \r\nKumawat, Ram Singh. 2020. Entomological Survey of Vectors of Scrub Typhus in Haulawng, Lunglei District, \r\nMizoram, India. Journal of Communicable Diseases, 52(2): 69-73.IF: 0.07 \r\n10.SN Sharma, Ram Singh, N Balakrishnan, Rina Kumawat, SK Singh. 2020. Vectors of Crimean-Congo Hemorrhagic \r\nFever (CCHF):Prevention and its Control. Journal of Communicable Diseases, 52(3): 22-26.IF: 0.07 \r\n11.Amarpal Singh Bhadauriya, Shilpi Dhan, Pankaj U. Ramteke, Sweta Bhan, Rina Kumawat, Ram Singh, Arun Chouhan. \r\n2020. Entomological Survey for Aedes Species at Deendayal Seaport, Kandla, Gujarat India during Pre-Monsoon Period, \r\n2018. Journal of Communicable Diseases, 52(4): 35-38.IF: 0.07 \r\n12.SN Sharma, BR Mane, Mahendra Sonar, Sanket Kulkarni, Rina Kumawat, Ram Singh, SK Singh. 2021. Entomological \r\nSurvey for CCHF Vectors in Palghar District of Maharashtra. Journal of Communicable Diseases, 53(1): 10-14. \r\nIF: 0.07 \r\n13.SN Sharma, Rina Kumawat, SK Singh. 2022. Entomological Sampling Strategy, Sampling Tools and Analytical \r\nTechniques with regard to Vector Borne Diseases: Indian Perspective. Journal of Communicable Diseases, 54(1): 117- \r\n123.IF: 0.07 \r\n14.SN Sharma, Rina Kumawat, SK Singh. 2022. Good Medical Entomology Laboratories Practices (GMELP) in India: A \r\nConcept Note. Journal of Communicable Diseases, 54(1): 150-155.IF: 0.07 \r\n15.SN Sharma, Rina Kumawat, SK Singh. 2022. Mass Gathering and Population Movement in India: Possible Risk of \r\nVector–Borne Diseases. Journal of Communicable Diseases, 54(2): 49-54.IF: 0.07 \r\n16.SN Sharma, Rina Kumawat, SK Singh. 2022. Risk Assessment and Impact of Floods on the Transmission of Vector \r\nBorne Diseases. Journal of Communicable Diseases, 54(3): 15-21.IF: 0.07 \r\n17.SN Sharma, Rina Kumawat, SK Singh. 2023. Learning Module on Vector Surveillance, Biology and Management \r\nStrategy for Vector Borne Diseases. Journal of Communicable Diseases, 55(3): 1-159.IF: 0.07 \r\n18.SN Sharma, Rina Kumawat, SK Singh. 2024. Training Module on Ticks, Mites, and Flea Borne Diseases for Medical \r\nEntomologists. Journal of Communicable Diseases, 56(4): 1-27.IF: 0.07 ', 'NA', 'NA'),
(4, 5, 'With work experience in the both the public & private health sector, a medical doctor with an MD in Community Medicine. \r\n\r\nJoined ICMR as a Scientist-E (Medical) from May 2021. Previously worked as an Assistant Professor in DM WIMS, Wayanad, and was formerly the Technical Expert - Epidemiology & Project Lead in a Govt. of India project in AMCHSS, SCTIMST Trivandrum, to set up a Regional Technical Resource Centre for Health Technology Assessment. Also worked as an Assistant Professor - Epidemiologist in Government Medical College Palakkad. \r\n\r\nPrior to obtaining the MD degree, worked for the Health Services Department, Govt. of Kerala, as an Assistant Surgeon, in varying capacities ranging from primary health care institutions, right up to the District Level. \r\n\r\nOther areas of interest include Qualitative Research, Health Economics, Public Health Ethics, Health Policy and Systems Research.', '- MD Community Medicine – Government Medical College Trivandrum under Kerala University of Health Sciences\r\n\r\n- MBBS (Bachelor of Medicine and Bachelor of Surgery) – Government Medical College Calicut, under University of Calicut\r\n\r\n- Certificate Course - WHO TDR - Implementation Research with a focus on Infectious Diseases of Poverty (IDP)\r\n\r\n- Certificate Course - Implementation Science School - 5 (Sept 2022 Cohort) - conducted by the Global Alliance for Chronic Diseases (GACD) \r\n', '1.  Pelzer PT, Seeley J, Sun FY, Tameris M, Tao L, Yanlin Z, Moosan H, Weerasuriya C, Asaria M, Jayawardana S, White RG, Harris RC. Potential implementation strategies, acceptability, and feasibility of new and repurposed TB vaccines. PLOS Glob Public Health. 2022 May 3;2(5):e0000076. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgph.0000076. PMID: 36962104; PMCID: PMC10021736.\r\n \r\n2.  Gladson V, Moosan H, Mathew S, P D. Clinical and Laboratory Diagnostic Features of Kyasanur Forest Disease: A Study From Wayanad, South India. Cureus. 2021 Dec 6;13(12):e20194. doi: 10.7759/cureus.20194. PMID: 35004016; PMCID: PMC8728626.\r\n\r\n3.  Sureshbabu S, Moosan H, Joseph M, Haseena CV, Lekshmi R, Naz A, Muralikrishnan VP, Mohanlal S, Alappat PJ, Tushar VP, Nayak D. Knowledge, Attitude, and Practice of epilepsy in Wayanad, Kerala. Epilepsy Behav. 2022 Aug;133:108762. doi: 10.1016/j.yebeh.2022.108762. Epub 2022 Jun 10. PMID: 35697588.\r\n\r\n4.  Moosan H, Stanley A, Vijayakumar K, Jayasree A, Lawrence T, Veena A. Impediments to optimal health-care utilization of a particularly vulnerable tribal group in Wayanad: A qualitative study. Indian J Community Med. 2019;44(5):62. .\r\n\r\n5.  Moosan H, Stanley A, Prabhakaran A, Vijayakumar K, Jayasree A, Gopakumar S. Comparison of health-care utilization pattern and its correlates among the tribal and nontribal population of Kerala. Indian J Community Med. 2019;44(5):57. .\r\n\r\n6.  Arjun P, Nair S, Jilisha G, Anand J, Babu V, Moosan H, Kumari AK. Assessing health-seeking behavior among Asthma and COPD patients in urban South India. J Family Med Prim Care. 2019 Aug 28;8(8):2714-2719. doi:10.4103/jfmpc.jfmpc_485_19. eCollection 2019 Aug. PubMed PMID: 31548962; PubMed Central PMCID: PMC6753802. .\r\n\r\n7.  Sanker P, Satheesan A, Ambika AP, Balakrishnan R, Moosan H, Mrithunjayan SK, et al. Role of line probe assay in detection of extra-pulmonary tuberculosis/multidrug-resistant tuberculosis: The experience from Kerala state, India. J Acad Clin Microbiol. 2017;19(1):12. .\r\n\r\n8.  Sanker P, Ambika AP, Santhosh VT, Kottuthodi RP, Balakrishnan R, Mrithunjayan SK, Moosan H. Are WHO approved nucleic acid amplification tests causing large-scale “false identification” of rifampicin-resistant tuberculosis: Programmatic experience from South India. Int J Mycobacteriology. 2017;6(1):21. \r\n\r\n9.  Moosan H, Nujum ZT. Theories and Modelling for Health Policy. Health Sci. 2016 Jul;1(3):28–34. .\r\n\r\n10.  V A, Rachel Philip R, Moosan H, Gopakumar S. Comparison of Prevalence of Anemia and Associated Factors among Adolescent Girls under WIFS with Non-WIFS Group in a School in North Kerala. Indian J Youth Adolesc Health. 2016;3(4):3–7. .\r\n\r\n11.  Bhat LD, Nayar KR, Moosan H, Nair S, Shaffi M. Hand-Washing and Public Health. Economic and Political Weekly. 2015;50(48):19. .\r\n\r\n12.  Nujum ZT, Tv A, K V, Ts A, Moosan H. A Framework for Healthcare Provision to Children with Intellectual Disability. Disabil CBR Incl Dev [Internet]. 2013 Feb 11 [cited 2017 Mar 20];23(4) .', '1. World Health Organization (WHO) funded Agreement to Perform Work (APW) project titled “Defining and Mapping of Implementation Research on Non-Communicable Diseases in India\"\r\n\r\n', '1. Capacity Building Training for Frontline Health Workers for Screening and Management Of Common Mental Health Disorders in Primary Care (Mission Mode Project)\r\n\r\n2. Spatial Epidemiological Approach to Malaria Elimination Research (SEAMER)');
INSERT INTO `emp_profile` (`id`, `emp_id`, `brief_intro`, `academic`, `publication`, `comp_project`, `ong_project`) VALUES
(5, 6, 'Dr. Ansuman Panigrahi is currently working as Scientist – E (Medical) at ICMR-NIIRNCD, Jodhpur (Rajasthan). Dr. Panigrahi was the former Professor, Community Medicine at Kalinga Institute of Medical Sciences (KIMS), KIIT University, Bhubaneswar (Odisha); Advisory board member of KIIT Centre of Excellence on Occupational Safety and Health; Founder member of Medical Education Unit, KIMS and Member Secretary of Institute Ethics Committee, KIMS. Dr. Panigrahi has received the prestigious “ICMR-Prize for Biomedical Research conducted in Underdeveloped Areas – 2019” and “Chinta O Chetana National Award-2022” along with various awards for his contribution in teaching, research and work in Community Medicine.', 'M.D. Community Medicine, 2006 – S.C.B. Medical College, Utkal University\r\nMBBS, 1999 – M.K.C.G. Medical College, Berhampur University', 'As first and/or corresponding author, Dr. Panigrahi has published > 40 research papers in International/National journals indexed in PubMed/Medline/Scopus/Web of Science/SCI/Embase database.\r\n\r\nResearch Publication:\r\n1. 	Samanta P, Panigrahi A, Senapati LK, Mishra DP, Ravan JR, Mishra J. Maladaptive Behavior and Associated Factors among Young Children with Autism. Indian J Pediatr. 2022;2–4. \r\n2. 	Dhal SS, Mohanty N, Mohakud NK, Panigrahi A, Sahoo M. Overt and Covert Delinquency: Socio-Demographic Correlates in Justice-Involved Adolescent Males. Int J Offender Ther Comp Criminol. 2022;0306624X2211028. \r\n3. 	Panigrahi A, Sharma D, Maharana S. Intention to Quit Tobacco Use and its Determinants among Adolescent Tobacco Users Residing in Slum Areas of Bhubaneswar, India: A Cross-Sectional Study. Indian J Med Paediatr Oncol. 2021;42(5):444–50. \r\n4. 	Panigrahi A, Nageswar Rao G, Kumari Konar A. Vision-Related Quality of Life and Its Sociodemographic Correlates Among Individuals With Visual Impairments. J Vis Impair Blind. 2021;115(4):319–28. \r\n5. 	Dash K, Panigrahi A, Pathak M, Jena PK. Smoking Prevalence , Attitude , and Cessation Training among Nursing Students in Bhubaneswar , India — A Cross-sectional Study. Indian J Med Paediatr Oncol. 2022; \r\n6. 	Mishra J, Panigrahi A, Samanta P, Dash K. Sleep quality and associated factors among undergraduate medical students during Covid-19 confinement. Clin Epidemiol Glob Heal. 2022;15(January). \r\n7. 	Panigrahi A, Sharma D, Maharana S. Intention to Quit Tobacco Use and its Determinants Among Adolescent Tobacco Users Residing in Slum Areas of Bhubaneswar , India : A Cross-Sectional Study. Indian J Med Paediatr Oncol. 2021;42(5):444–50. \r\n8. 	Kande S, Patro S, Panigrahi A, Khora PK, Pattnaik D. Prevalence of Uropathogens and Their Antimicrobial Resistance Pattern among Adult Diabetic Patients. Indian J Public Health. 2021;65:280–6. \r\n9. 	Mishra J, Samanta P, Panigrahi A, Dash K, Behera MR, Das R. Mental Health Status, Coping Strategies During Covid-19 Pandemic Among Undergraduate Students of Healthcare Profession. Int J Ment Health Addict [Internet]. 2021;(0123456789). Available from: https://doi.org/10.1007/s11469-021-00611-1\r\n10. 	Panigrahi A, Mohapatra I, Kanyari S, Maharana S, Panigrahi M. Impact of environmental temperature and relative humidity on spread of COVID-19 infection in India : A cross-sectional time-series analysis. Arch Environ Occup Heal [Internet]. 2021; Available from: https://doi.org/10.1080/19338244.2021.1910117\r\n11. 	Raman S, Mishra P, Panigrahi A, Rout N, Dash K. Glomerular C4d deposition in proliferative glomerular diseases. Indian J Pathol Microbiol. 2021;64(1):69–77. \r\n12. 	Bhutada T, Ray AK, Dash BK, Panigrahi A. Serum cholinesterase level in postmortem cases of pesticide poisoning : Devising a protocol for identifying exposure to organophosphate and carbamate compounds. J Indian Acad Forensic Med. 2020;42(4):155–60. \r\n13. 	Samanta P, Bhattacharyya D, Panigrahi A, Mishra S, Mishra J, Senapati LK. Body composition changes and its association with dyslipidemia in patients receiving hemodialysi