(7, 8, NULL, '- M.Sc. from Dr. B. R. Ambedkar University, Khandari Campus Agra: Zoology (Specialization-Environmental Biology)\r\n- Ph.D. from Dr. B. R. Ambedkar University, Khandari Campus Agra: Zoology\r\n', '1.	SK Yadav, V Bijalwan, S Yadav, K Sarkar, S Das, DP Singh; Susceptibility of male reproductive system to bisphenol-A, an endocrine disruptor: Updates from epidemiological and experimental evidence. Journal of Biochemical and Molecular Toxicology,  https://doi.org/10.1002/jbt.23292\r\n2.	Yadav SK, Patil GP, Virmagami A, et al. Occupational lead exposure is an independent modulator of hypertension and poor pulmonary function: A cross-sectional comparative study in lead-acid battery recycling workers. Toxicology and Industrial Health. 2022;38(3):139-150. doi:10.1177/07482337221076248\r\n3.	Murhekar MV, Bhatnagar T, Thangaraj JWV, Saravanakumar V, Santhosh Kumar M, Selvaraju S, et al. (2021) Seroprevalence of IgG antibodies against SARS-CoV-2 among the general population and healthcare workers in India, June– July 2021: A population-based cross-sectional study. PLoS Med 18(12): e1003877. https://doi. org/10.1371/journal.pmed.1003877\r\n4.	Murhekar MV, Bhatnagar T, Thangaraj et.al. (2021) SARS-Cov-2 Seroprevalence among the general populations and healthcare workers in India, December 2020-January 2021. International Journal of Infectious Diseases (108) 145-155 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijid.2021.05.040  \r\n5.	Murhekar MV, Bhatnagar T, Selvaraju  et. al. Prevalence of SARS-CoV-2 infection in India: Findings from the national serosurvey, May-June 2020. Indian J Med Res 2020;152: 48-60. \r\n6.	Palkhade R, Yadav S, Mishra S, Muhamed J (2018) Acute oral toxicity of pesticide combination (acephate 50% and imidacloprid 1.8% as active ingredients) in Sprague-Dawley rats, Veterinary World, 11(9): 1291-1297. \r\n7.	R Palkhade, P Barfal, S Yadav, Awandkar SP, A. Pagdhune (2018). Epididymo-Orchitis in a Veterinary Physician: A Case of Occupational Brucellosis. J Foodborne and Zoonotic Dis, 6(1):05-07 \r\n8.	Majumder J, Bagepally BS, Shah P, Kotadiya S, Yadav S & Naha N “Comparison of worker’s perceptions toward work climate and health symptoms between ceramic and iron foundry workers. Indian Journal of Occupational & Environmental Medicine, 2016; 20:48-53. \r\n9.	J Majumder, P Shah, BS Bagepally (2016) Task distribution, work environment, and perceived health discomforts among Indian ceramic workers, American Journal of Industrial Medicine, 59 (12), 1145-1155 \r\n10.	SP Yadav, S Yadav, P Kumar & S Yadav “Knowledge, treatment-seeking behaviour and socio-economic impact of malaria in the desert of Rajasthan, India” South African Journal of Epidemiology & Infections 2013; 28 (1):41-47. \r\n11.	S Yadav, SP Singh & PK Mittal “Toxicity of Thevetia peruviana (Yellow oleander) against larvae of Anopheles stephensi and Aedes aegypti vectors of malaria and dengue”. Journal of Entomology and Zoology Studies 2013; 1 (6): 85-87. \r\n12.	S. Yadav, P. K. Mittal, A.P. Dash and P.N. Saxena “Effect of synergist Piperonyl Butaoxide (PBO) on the toxicity of some essential oils against mosquito larvae” Journal of Communicable Diseases, 40 (4) 2008: 263-268 \r\n13.	Mohini Tiwary, S. N. Naik, D. K. Tiwary, P. K. Mittal and S. Yadav. Chemical composition and larvicidal activities of the essential oil of Zanthoxylum armatum DC (Rutaceae) against three mosquito vectors. Journal of Vector Borne Diseases, 44, September 2007, pp. 198-204  \r\n14.	P. K.  Mittal, S. Yadav, C. P. Batra and   T.  Adak. Laboratory evaluation of phenthoate against Anopheles culicifacies, Anopheles stephensi (malaria vectors) and Culex quinquefasciatus and investigation of its cross resistance with malathion in An. stephensi. Pesticide Research Journal, June, 2007 ;19(1): 79-81\r\n15.	M. F. Alam, A. K. Chopra, V. K. Dua and S. Yadav, Bio-Environmental Approach for Vector Control. Asian J. Exp. Sci., Vol. 20, No. 2, 2006, 387-398\r\n16.	Satya Vir & Suresh Yadav. Bioefficacy of neem oil as seed protectant of mungbean, mothbean, cowpea and urd against Callosobruchus maculates Fabricus. J. Arid Legumes 1(2): 118-121.', 'As Principal Investigator\r\n1.	District Level Household and Facility Survey-4-CAB Components in (Gujarat State & 2 UT)-   Funded by NIHFW (GOI)\r\n2.	Study the prevalence of underlying silicosis among tuberculosis and drug resistant tuberculosis cases of glass industry workers of Firozabad (UP) district.\r\n3.	Study of occupational risks and biochemical changes among adolescents related with waste recycling industry processes in Gujarat.\r\n4.	Assessment of occupational environmental health risks and challenges of migratory brick kiln workers in Gujarat.\r\n5.	Elucidating the Effects of Bisphenol-A on Male Reproductive System of Mice and Exploration of Mechanism using Toxico-proteomics Approach- ICMR-PDF\r\nAs Co-Principal Investigator\r\n\r\n1.	Defining and Mapping Implementation Research on Cardiovascular Disease, diabetes mellitus, and Mental ailments in India.: A systematic review- Extramural\r\n2.	An Intervention study for the promotion of ‘COVID-19 Appropriate Behaviour’ using culturally and socially appropriate methods among rural Population- Extramural\r\n3.	National Survey for the state-wise prevalence of Pulmonary Tuberculosis in India- Extramural\r\n4.	Environment determinants of SARS CoV-2 transmission and spread of Mucor mycosis: an exploratory study- Extramural\r\n5.	Assessment of Iodine Status among Pregnant & Lactating Women and 6-11 Year Children in Selected District of Rajasthan- Extramural\r\n6.	Nationwide study to estimate incidence, mortality, morbidity, and economic burden due to snakebites in India- Extramural\r\n7.	Health status of workers in ceramic industries and iron foundries- Funded by DISH Gujarat- Extramural\r\n8.	Spatial analysis of the association between air pollution, cancer and selected NCDs’ and their risk factors in India-Intramural \r\n9.	Study of brucellosis disease prevalence and occupational risk factors among high-risk groups of veterinarians and para-veterinarians along with dairy farm workers in endemic districts of Gujarat-Intramural\r\n10.	Toxicological Evaluation of Acephate and Imidacloprid as combination pesticide practiced by farmers in rat.- Intramural\r\n', 'As Principal Investigator\r\n1.	Estimation of disease burden and Investigation of the Risk Factors associated with Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) in selected regions of Rajasthan-Extramural \r\n2.	ICMR Task Force study on Epidemiology of Chronic Respiratory Illness in Select Population Groups in India (CRISPI)- Extramural\r\n3.	A demonstration Project for Reduction of Tuberculosis in India-Mega TB Elimination- Extramural\r\n4.	Longitudinal Silicosis Study Balesar (LSSB) among silica exposed sand stone mine workers in Balesar, Jodhpur- Intramural\r\n\r\nAs Co-Principal Investigator\r\n1.	Development and Validation of Artificial Intelligence Tool for Screening/detection of Pulmonary TB and other lung diseases using Chest X-rays.\r\n'),
(8, 9, 'Dr. Ramesh Kumar Huda is currently working as Scientist-C (IT) of ICMR-NIIRNCD, Jodhpur. Previously he was working as an Assistant Professor of Computer Science and Engineering at MBM Engineering College (JNVU), Jodhpur. He has done his B.Tech in Computer Science and Engineering (CSE) from Rajasthan Technical University, Kota and M.Tech & Ph.D. in CSE from IIT, Dhanbad. Dr. Huda has authored and co-authored research papers published in various reputed journals.', '- B.Tech (Computer Science & Engineering - RTU Kota) \r\n\r\n- M.Tech  (Computer Science & Engineering - IIT Dhanbad) \r\n\r\n- Ph.D. (Machine Learning -IIT Dhanbad)', 'Please refer to the Google Scholar link provided above.', 'Sr. No.	                 Project Name\r\n1. Study to evaluate the effectiveness of BCG vaccine in Reducing Morbidity in Elderly individuals in COVID-19 Hotspots in India. (As Co-PI)\r\n\r\n2. World Health Organization (WHO) funded the Agreement to Perform Work (APW) project titled “Defining and Mapping of Implementation Research on Non-Communicable Diseases in India\" (As Co-PI)\r\n\r\n3. Strengthening state non-communicable disease programme for early detection of breast cancer involving strategic education and awareness among women. (As Co-PI)\r\n\r\n4. Spatial analysis of the association between air pollution, cancer and selected NCDs’ and their risk factors in India (As PI)\r\n\r\n5. Screening Of Tribal Population For Sickle Cell Anemia In Abu Road & Pindwara Blocks Of Sirohi District, Gogunda Block Of Udaipur District and In Kishanganj And Sahabad Blocks Of Baran District (As PI)\r\n\r\n6. Jodhpur urban air quality monitoring project (As PI)\r\n\r\n7. Social Determinants of Low Birth Weight: A Hospital-Based Study (As Co-PI)\r\n\r\n8. Estimation of disease burden and Investigation of the Risk Factors associated with chronic kidney disease (CKD) in selected districts of Rajasthan (As Co-PI)\r\n\r\n9. Screening of Maabadi and Hostel Residents for Sickle Cell Diseases in Tribal Blocks of Rajasthan : A collaborative Implementation Research Project (As Co-PI)\r\n\r\n10. SMS and Android application based Intervention in Diabetes: A Community-based Intervention study (As PI)\r\n\r\n11. Evidence Synthesis for Lung Cancer Guidelines: Conducting Systematic reviews / Meta-Analysis (Prevention : Air pollution) (As Co-PI)\r\n', 'Sr. No.	                 Project Name\r\n\r\n1. NHRP-An Implementation Research to strengthen DIGItal technologies for optimizing the Continuum of CARE for Diabetes and Hypertension Management in the Public Healthcare System in India (DIGI-CARE) (As PI)\r\n\r\n2. Acceptance of digital medical tools and telemedicine in hard-to-reach areas as augmentation of conventional physical clinics and face-to-face consultation - An interventional study (As PI)\r\n\r\n3. Implementing a Screening and Identification Model for Latent Tuberculosis Among Family Members of TB Patients Using the Cytb Test with Android Application (As PI)\r\n\r\n4. Improving diagnosis and management of frailty using IoT in primary health care Setting: A proof-of-concept study (As PI)\r\n\r\n5. Capacity building training of frontline workers (doctors & nurses) for screening & management of mental disorders in primary care (mission mode project) (As Co-PI)\r\n\r\n6. Implementation Research on Enhancing Trauma Care at Health and Wellness Centres (As Co-PI)\r\n\r\n7. High population-based coverage of high-risk pregnancy identification and management through effective antenatal care and care continuum across antenatal, intrapartum and postnatal care- An Implementation Research (As Co-PI)\r\n\r\n8. Implementation research on primordial prevention of NCDs among school-going children (As Co-PI)\r\n\r\n9. Nomophobia Prevention and Behavioural Therapy: An Interventional Study Among College-going Adolescents and Young Adults in Selected Districts of Rajasthan, Haryana and Himachal Pradesh (As Co-PI)\r\n\r\n10. Optimizing Adolescent Healthcare Services Through Health and Wellness Centres in Sikar District of Rajasthan: An Implementation Research (As Co-PI)\r\n');
INSERT INTO `emp_profile` (`id`, `emp_id`, `brief_intro`, `academic`, `publication`, `comp_project`, `ong_project`) VALUES
(9, 10, 'Dr. Ramesh Kumar Sangwan is currently working as Scientist-C at the National Institute for Implementation Research on Non-Communicable Diseases in Jodhpur, Rajasthan. In addition to his current position, he has taught at Government College, Loharu, Bhiwani, and the Department of Economics at Maharshi Dayanand University, Rohtak, Haryana. He has also worked as an Adolescent Health Counsellor at the General Hospital in Charkhi Dadri and as a Social Worker at the Integrated Child Protection Scheme in Bhiwani, Haryana.\r\nDr. Ramesh Kumar Sangwan has received the University Research Scholarship for his Doctoral study and a grant for the publication of his Doctoral Thesis from the Indian Council of Social Science Research in New Delhi. He has actively participated in numerous national and international conferences where he presented research papers. Furthermore, he has published several papers in various journals and has contributed to edited books. Notably, he has authored books titled \"Health, Human Rights and Ethics,\" \"Health and Society,\" and \"Health, Hygiene, and Lifestyle.\"', 'Post-Graduation from the Department of Sociology, Maharshi Dayanand University, Rohtak, Haryana.\r\nM.Phil from the Department of Sociology, Maharshi Dayanand University, Rohtak, Haryana.\r\nPh.D. from the Department of Sociology, from Maharshi Dayanand University, Rohtak, Haryana.', '1.	Huda RK, Chowhan Rahul S, Arora S, Seervi D, Verma S, Singh J, Kalra S, Choudhary A, Sangwan RK, et al. (2025) Perceptions of healthcare workers and patients on the implementation of telemedicine in hard-to-reach areas: A qualitative study from Rajasthan, India. DIGITAL HEALTH. 2025;11. doi:10.1177/20552076251331874. IF: 2.9\r\n2.	Huda R K, Singh J, Sangwan RK, et al. (2025) Understanding Diabetes Through the Patients’ Lens: A Mixed-Methods Study on Experiences and Outcomes in Urban Areas of Rajasthan, India. Cureus 17(3): e81218. doi:10.7759/cureus.81218. IF: 1.2\r\n3.	Sangwan RK, Kansara D, Matoria S, Ali H, Khetan M, Singh V, Thakor M, Huda RK and Babu BV (2024) Perceptions of primary healthcare providers for screening and management of mental health disorders in India: a qualitative study. Front. Public Health. 12:1446606. doi: 10.3389/fpubh.2024.1446606, IF: 3\r\n4.	Arora, S., Huda, R.K., Verma, S., Khetan, M., & Sangwan, R.K. (2024). Challenges, Barriers, and Facilitators in Telemedicine Implementation in India: A Scoping Review. Cureus 16(8): e67388. DOI 10.7759/cureus.67388, IF: 1.2\r\n5.	Daniel Bella Devaleenal, Venkatesan Mythily, Padmapriyadarsin Chandrasekaran, ..Sangwan Rk, … et al. Safety profile of BCG revaccination for COVID prevention among elderly individuals in India, Indian Journal of Tuberculosis, Available online 10 May 2024, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijtb.2024.05.003. IF: 0.97\r\n6.	Saha, Indranil; Kundu, Ramendra Nath; Ghosh, Anuska; Chetri, Birshikha; Sangwan Ramesh Kumar; Saha, Asim; Chakrabarti, Amit; Sharma, Arun Kumar. IJCM_213A: Feasibility and acceptability of different rapid methods for estimation of potentially missed mortality events during COVID-19 pandemic time in West Bengal. Indian Journal of Community Medicine 49(Suppl1):p S62, April 2024. DOI:10.4103/ijcm.ijcm_abstract213, IF: 0.9\r\n7.	Sangwan RK, Kansara D, Huda RK, Kumar P. Factors Influencing Rising Caesarean Section: A Narrative Review of Quantitative Studies, IJAR Volume– 14, Issue– 01, January 2024, PRINT ISSN No 2249 - 555X.\r\n8.	Sangwan RK, Huda RK, Panigrahi A, Toteja GS, Sharma AK, Thakor M and Kumar P (2023) Strengthening breast cancer screening program through health education of women and capacity building of primary healthcare providers. Front. Public Health 11:1276853. doi: 10.3389/fpubh.2023.1276853, IF: 3\r\n9.	Murhekar MV, Bhatnagar T, Thangaraj JWV, Saravanakumar V, Santhosh Kumar M, Selvaraju S, Rade K, Kumar CPG, Sabarinathan R, Asthana S, Balachandar R, Bangar SD, Bansal AK, Bhat J, Chakraborty D, Chopra V, Das D, Devi KR, Dwivedi GR, Jain A, Khan SMS, Kumar MS, Laxmaiah A, Madhukar M, Mahapatra A, Ramesh T, Rangaraju C, Turuk J, Yadav S, Bhargava B; ICMR serosurveillance group. Seroprevalence of IgG antibodies against SARS-CoV-2 among the general population and healthcare workers in India, June- July 2021: A population-based cross-sectional study. PLoS Med. 2021 Dec 10;18(12):e1003877. doi: 10.1371/journal.pmed.1003877. PMID: 34890407; PMCID: PMC8726494, IF: 15.28\r\n10.	Murhekar MV, Bhatnagar T, Sangwan Ramesh Kumar et al. SARS-CoV-2 antibody seroprevalence in India, August–September, 2020: findings from the second nationwide household serosurvey. The Lancet Global Health. March 01, 2021; Vol. 9, ISSUE 3, E257- E266, IF: 19.9\r\n11.	Sangwan Ramesh Kumar & Kumar Devinder (2019) Health and Society, Academic Publication: New Delhi.\r\n12.	Sangwan Ramesh Kumar (2017) Health, Human Rights and Ethics: A Sociological Study in Hospital Setting, Rawat Publications: Jaipur.\r\n13.	Sangwan Ramesh Kumar, The Ethics of Caring: A Study of Doctors in Hospital Settings. International Journal of Science, Technology & Society. July-December, 2015, Vol. I, No.II.\r\n14.	Sangwan Ramesh Kumar (2015) Health Hygiene & Lifestyle, Academic Publication: New Delhi.\r\n15.	Sangwan Ramesh Kumar, Patient as Knower of Health Rights. IJPHRM. September, 2015  Vol.- II No. II.\r\n16.	Sangwan Ramesh Kumar (2015) “Health Rights & Healthcare Challenges”, in Swati Chakraborty (eds.) Multidisciplinary Handbook of Social Exclusion & Human Rights, Aayu  Publications, New Delhi.\r\n17.	Sangwan Ramesh Kumar (2015) “Personal Hygiene, Birth Practice & Change in Marriage Age”, in Abdul Matin (eds.) Development Health & Globalization, Academic Publication  New Delhi.\r\n18.	Sangwan Ramesh Kumar, Health Rights of Patients & Medical Ethics. Journal of Social Research. January, 2012 Vol.- VIII No.1.\r\n19.	Sangwan Ramesh Kumar, Health Hygiene & Lifestyle Behaviour. Journal of Social Research. July, 2011, Vol.- VII No. II.\r\n', '•	A multi-site study on feasibility, acceptability, and validity of different rapid methods for estimation of potentially missed events to measure COVID-19 associated mortality including mortalities associated with few selected comorbidities in India: Principal Investigator.\r\n•	Social Determinants of Low Birth Weight: A Hospital-Based Study: Principal Investigator.\r\n•	Estimation of disease burden and Investigation of the Risk Factors associated with Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) in selected regions of Rajasthan: Co-PI.\r\n•	Screening of tribal population for Sickle Cell Anemia in selected blocks of Sirohi, Udaipur and Baran District:  Principal Investigator.\r\n•	Strengthening State Non-Communicable Disease Programme for Early Detection of Breast Cancer Involving Strategic Education and Awareness Among Women” a joint program of ICMR-NIIRNCD and State Government: Principal Investigator.\r\n•	Study to Evaluate the Effectiveness of BCG Vaccine in Reducing Morbidity in Elderly Individuals in COVID-19 Hotspots in India: Co-PI.\r\n•	An Intervention study for the promotion of ‘COVID-19 Appropriate Behaviour’ using culturally and socially appropriate methods among the rural population: Co-PI.\r\n•	Nationwide Study to Estimate Incidence, Mortality, Morbidity, and Economic Burden Due to Snakebites in India: Co-PI.\r\n\r\n\r\n', '1.	Optimizing Adolescent Healthcare Services Through Health and Wellness Centres in Sikar District of Rajasthan: An Implementation Research: Principal Investigator\r\n2.	Capacity Building Training for Frontline Health Workers for Screening and Management of Common Mental Health Disorders in Primary Care: Principal Investigator.\r\n3.	High Population-Based Coverage of High-Risk Pregnancy Identification and Management: Co-PI\r\n4.	Implementation Research on Enhancing Trauma Care at Health and Wellness Centres: Co-PI\r\n5.	Implementation Research on Primordial Prevention of NCDs Among School-Going Children: Co-PI\r\n6.	SMS and Android application-based Intervention in Diabetes: A Community-based Intervention Study: Co-PI\r\n7.	Acceptance of digital medical tools and telemedicine in hard-to-reach areas as augmentation of conventional physical clinics and face-to-face consultation: An interventional study: Co-PI.\r\n8.	An Implementation Research to strengthen DIGItal technologies for optimizing the Continuum of CARE for Diabetes and Hypertension Management in the Public Healthcare System in India (DIGI-CARE): Co-PI\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),
(10, 11, 'Dr. Mahendra J Thakor has Joined ICMR –National Institute for Implementation Research on Non-Communicable Diseases, Jodhpur, Rajasthan (formerly Desert Medicine Research Centre) as Scientist B(Medical) in the year 2019. He has experience in the field of BA BE (Bioavailability & Bioequivalence) clinical drug trials, Occupational health problems, Sickle cell disease, and NCD diseases.', 'M.B.B.S. (2009) from Shree M.P Shah Govt. medical college, Jamnagar\r\nAssociate Fellow of Industrial Health from MGLI & NIOH.', '1. Seroprevalence of IgG antibodies against SARS-CoV-2 among the general population and healthcare workers in India, June-July 2021: A population-based cross-sectional study\r\n2.Moosan H, Thakor M, Sharma AK et al. Protocol of systematic reviews on implementation research on cardiovascular diseases, diabetes mellitus and mental ailments in India [version 1; peer review: 1 approved]. F1000Research 2023, 12:100 (https://doi.org/10.12688/f1000research.128549.1)\r\n3.Suman Sundar Mohanty Chetram Meena,Mahendra Thakor et al.Model rural health research unit’s NS1‐based serosurveillance recommends for vector control in rural villages of Jaipur district, Rajasthan, India', '1. Study to evaluate the effectiveness of BCG vaccine in Reducing Morbidity in Elderly individuals in COVID-19 Hotspots in India.(As Site-PI)\r\n2. Defining and Mapping Implementation Research on Cardiovascular Disease, diabetes mellitus, and Mental ailments in India.: A systematic review(As Co-PI)\r\n3. A Single-arm Feasibility Trial of Community-based Pulmonary Rehabilitation for Adults with COPD in slum areas of Jodhpur, Rajasthan”(As Co-PI)\r\n4. Strengthening state noncommunicable disease program for early detection of breast cancer involving strategic education and awareness among women.(As Co-PI)\r\n', '1. Estimation of disease burden and Investigation of the Risk Factors associated with chronic kidney disease (CKD) in selected districts of Rajasthan(As Co-PI)\r\n2. Screening Of Tribal Population For Sickle Cell Anemia In Abu Road & Pindwara Blocks Of Sirohi District, Gogunda Block Of Udaipur District, And In Kishanganj And Sahabad Blocks Of Baran District(As Co-PI)\r\n3. Assessment of neonatal screening approaches for sickle cell disease and effect of early intervention in the management of disease in tribal populations.(As Co-PI)\r\n4. Social Determinants of Low Birth Weight: A Hospital-Based Study(As Co-PI)\r\n5. Spatial Epidemiological Approach to Malaria Elimination Research (SEAMER)(As Co-PI)\r\n6.Screening of Maabadi and Hostel Residents for Sickle Cell Diseases in Tribal Blocks of Rajasthan : A collaborative Implementation Research Project(As Co-PI)\r\n'),
(11, 12, 'Dr Vishal Singh is currently working as Scientist – B at ICMR-NIIRNCD, Jodhpur, Rajasthan. He has a Ph.D. (2021) in Biochemistry, specializing in nanotherapeutics development for Type-2 Diabetes. Dr. Vishal Singh has eight years of research experience in developing nanotherapeutics and nanodiagnostics for chronic diseases. He has received various fellowship awards during his research career including an ICMR-JRF, CSIR-UGC-NET, M.K Bhan Fellowship, and Eyes High Postdoctoral Match-Funding Fellowship at UCalgary, Canada. He is currently focused on developing Point-of-Care systems and utilizing AI for early screening and better management of NCDs. Presently, Dr. Vishal Singh is in-charge of the Biochemistry laboratory at ICMR-NIIRNCD.\r\n\r\n\r\n', 'Educational Qualifications\r\n1. Ph.D. (Biochemistry, 2021): Panjab University, Chandigarh & National Agri-Food \r\n    Biotechnology Institute, Mohali, India\r\n2. M.Sc. (Hons.) (Biochemistry, 2014): Panjab University, Chandigarh, India.                                                                                                                                                                               3. B.Sc. (Hons.) (Biochemistry, 2012): Panjab University, Chandigarh, India.\r\n\r\nResearch Experience\r\n1. Scientist-B: 07-03-2022 to ongoing\r\n2. MK Bhan Fellow: 13-10-2021 to 03-03-2022\r\n3. NABI-Senior Research Fellow: 16-07-2021 to 24-09-2021\r\n4. PhD scholar: 23-02-2016 to 18-12-2020\r\n\r\nTrainings\r\n1. Basic Biomethodology of Laboratory Animals at IMTECH, Chandigarh, India.\r\n2. NANOTECHNOLOGY: Nanoparticle Synthesis and Conjugation Chemistry for Bioapplications at GJUST, Hisar, India\r\n3. IWAM: International Workshop on Advanced Materials, Ras Al Khaimah, UAE.\r\n\r\nAwards\r\n1. Eyes High Postdoctoral Match-Funding Fellowship for 2022-2023 with an annual salary \r\n     of $50,000 at UCalgary, Canada. \r\n2. MK Bhan-Young Researcher Fellowship Program (YRFP) for 2020-21 session. \r\n3. Excellence award on “National Workshop on Basic Biomethodology for Laboratory \r\n    Animals” at IMTECH, Chandigarh India. \r\n5. Best poster prize at “CHINTAN-2018” held at NABI, Mohali, India.\r\n6. ICMR-JRF\r\n7. CSIR-UGC-NET\r\n8. Merit certificate from Panjab University in M.Sc. (Hons.) Biochemistry.\r\n\r\n\r\n', '1. Sharma, S.K., Mohanty, B.P., Singh, V., Bansal, M.P., Singhal, N.K., Sharma, S.K., Sandhir, R., 2023. Trace elements dyshomeostasis in liver and brain of weanling mice under altered dietary selenium conditions. Journal of Trace Elements in Medicine and Biology. 80: 127305. (IF-3.5)\r\n2. Gupta, R., Singh, V., Sarawagi, N., Kaur, G., Kaur, R., Priyadarshi, N., Rishi, V., Goyal, B,, Mishra, PP,, Singhal, NK., 2023. Salmonella typhimurium detection and ablation using OmpD specific aptamer with non-magnetic and magnetic graphene oxide. Biosensors & Bioelectronics. 234:115354. (I.F- 12.6)\r\n3. Gupta, S., Singh, V., Subramaniam, G., Singhal, NK., Sandhir, R., 2021. siRNA mediated GSK3β knockdown targets insulin signaling pathway and rescues Alzheimer’s disease pathology: Evidence from in vitro and in vivo studies. Applied Materials and Interfaces (ACS), 14, 1, 69–93. (I.F-9.5)\r\n4. Sagar, P., Singh, V., Gupta, R., Kaul, S., Sharma, S., Kaur, S., Bhunia, R., Kondepudi, KK., Singhal, NK., 2021. pH triggered, synbiotic hydrogel beads for in vivo therapy of iron deficiency anemia and reduced inflammatory response. Applied Bio Materials (ACS), 4, 10, 7467–7484. (I.F-4.7)\r\n5. Gupta, R., Kaul, S., Singh, V., Sandeep, K., Singhal, NK., 2021. Graphene Oxide and Fluorescent aptamer based novel biosensor for detection of 25-hydroxyvitamin D3. Scientific Reports, 11, 23456. (I.F-4.6)\r\n6. Singh, V., Sagar, P., Kaul, S., Sandhir, R., Singhal, NK., 2020. Liver Phosphoenolpyruvate Carboxykinase-1 Downregulation via siRNA Functionalized Graphene Oxide Nanosheets Restores Glucose Homeostasis in Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus in-vivo Model. Bioconjugate Chemistry (ACS), 32, 2, 259–278. (IF- 4.7)\r\n7. Singh, V., Sandhir, R., Singhal, NK., 2020. Synthesis, characterization and toxicity profiling of graphene oxide-metformin hydrogel as a sustained release system for metformin in-vitro. Materials Today: Proceedings, 36, 3,769-774. (IF- 2.59)\r\n8. Kongot, M., Reddy, D., Singh, V., Patel, R., Singhal, NK., Kumar, A., 2020. Oxidovanadium (IV) and iron (III) complexes with O2N2 donor linkage as plausible antidiabetic candidates: Synthesis, structural characterizations, glucose uptake and model biological media studies. Applied Organometallic Chemistry. 34 (2), e5327. (IF- 4.072)\r\n9. Reddy, D., Kongot, M., Singh, V., Maurya, N., Patel, R., Singhal, NK., Avecilla, F., Kumar, A., 2019. Coumarin tethered cyclic imides as efficacious glucose uptake agents and investigation of hit candidate to probe its binding mechanism with human serum albumin. Bioorganic chemistry, 92, 103212. (IF- 5.1)\r\n10. Kongot, M., Reddy, D., Singh, V., Patel, R., Singhal, NK., Kumar, A., 2020. A manganese (II) complex tethered with S-benzyldithiocarbazate Schiff base: Synthesis, characterization, in-vitro therapeutic activity and protein interaction studies. Spectrochimica Acta Part A: Molecular and Biomolecular Spectroscopy, 231, 118123. (IF- 4.4)\r\n11. Kongot, M., Reddy, D., Singh, V., Patel, R., Singhal, NK., Kumar, A., 2020. Physicochemical, in-vitro therapeutic activity and biomolecular interaction studies of Mn(II), Ni(II) and Cu(II) complexes tethered with O2N2 ligand backbone. Spectrochimica Acta Part A: Molecular and Biomolecular Spectroscopy, 241, 118613. (IF- 4.4)\r\n12. Reddy, D., Kongot, M., Singh, V., Siddiquee, MA., Patel, R., Singhal, NK., Avecilla, F., Kumar, A., 2020. Biscoumarin–pyrimidine conjugates as potent anticancer agents and binding mechanism of hit candidate with human serum albumin. Archiv der Pharmazie, 354, 1, 2000181. (IF- 5.1)\r\n13. Nehra, M., Dilbaghi, A.N., Singh, V., Singhal, NK., Kumar, S., 2020. Highly Ordered and Crystalline Cu Nanowires in Anodic Aluminum Oxide Membranes for Biomedical Applications. physica status solidi a. 217, 13. (IF- 2.0)\r\n14. Kongot, M., Reddy, D., Singh, V., Patel, R., Singhal, NK., Kumar, A., 2019. ONS donor entwined iron(III) and cobalt(III) complexes with exemplary safety profile as potent anticancer and glucose uptake agents. New J. Chem. 43. (IF- 3.3)\r\n15. Singh, V., Kaul, S., Singla, P., Kumar, V., Sandhir, R., Chung, J.H., Garg, P., Singhal, N., 2018. Xylanase Immobilization on magnetic and magnetic Core/Shell nanocomposites using two different flexible alkyl length organophosphonates: linker length and shell effect on enzyme catalytic activity. Int J Biol Macromol. 115, 590-599. (IF- 8.2)\r\n16. Kongot, M., Dohare, N., Singh, V., Reddy, DS., Singhal, NK., Patel, R., Kumar, A., 2018. A novel biocompatible NiII tethered moiety as a glucose uptake agent and a hit against methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Eur J Pharm Sci. 123,335-349. (IF- 5.0)\r\n17. Kumari, A., Kaila, P., Tiwari, P., Singh, V., Kaul, S., Singhal, N., Guptasarma, P., 2018. Multiple thermostable enzyme hydrolases on magnetic nanoparticles: An immobilized enzyme-mediated approach to saccharification through simultaneous xylanase, cellulase and amylolytic glucanotransferase action. Int J Biol Macromol. 120, 1650-1658. (IF- 8.2)\r\n18. Kongot, M., Reddy, D., Singh, V., Patel, R., Singhal, NK., Kumar, A., 2018. Potent drug candidature of an ONS donor tethered copper (II) complex: Anticancer activity, cytotoxicity and spectroscopically approached BSA binding studies. Spectrochimica Acta Part A: Molecular and Biomolecular Spectroscopy 212, 330-342. (IF- 4.4)\r\n19. Patel, S.N ., Singh, V., Sharma, M., Sangwan, R.S., Singhal, N.K., Singh S.P., 2017.Development of a thermo-stable and recyclable magnetic nanaobiocatalyst for bioprocessing of fruit processing residues and D-allulose synthesis. Bioresource Technology, 247, 633–639. (IF- 11.4)\r\n20. Singh, V., Rakshit, K., Rathee, S., Angmo, S., Kaushal, S., Garg, P., Chung, J.H., Sandhir, R., Sangwan, R.S., Singhal, N., 2016. Metallic/bimetallic magnetic nanoparticle functionalization for immobilization of α-amylase for enhanced reusability in bio-catalytic processes. Bioresource Technology, 214, 528–533. (IF- 11.4)\r\n\r\nBook Chapter/s\r\n21. Singh, V., Kaushal, S., Singhal, NK., 2019. Applications of Nanoagrochemicals: Precepts and Prospects (Book chapter) in Emerging Trends in Nanobiomedicine: Nanoagroceuticals and Nanophytochemicals. 1-14.\r\n22. Kaul, S., Singh, V., Sandhir, R., Singhal, NK., 2020. Organophosphonate functionalized Au/Si@ Fe3O4: Versatile carrier for enzyme immobilization. Methods in Enzymology. 630, 199-214.', '1. A Single-arm Feasibility Trial of Community-based Pulmonary Rehabilitation for Adults with COPD in slum areas of Jodhpur, Rajasthan”- Co-PI\r\n2. Feasibility of utilization of Community Health Officers (CHOs) in the screening of Oral pre-malignant lesions (OPML) in Rajasthan- Co-PI', '1. Electrochemical Immunobiosensor-based Point-of-Care for Sickle Cell Disease/Trait and β-thalassemia. - PI\r\n2. Capacity Building Training for Frontline Health Workers for Screening and Management of Common Mental Health Disorders in Primary Care. - Co-PI'),
(12, 13, 'Dr. Janesh Gautam joined ICMR- NIIRNCD as Scientist-B in March 2022. Dr. Gautam has completed his Ph.D. and MSc in Biotechnology from Jawaharlal Nehru University, Delhi. He has more than 10 years of research experience and guided MSc and Ph.D. students during his Postdoc and Ph.D. Previously, He has worked as Assistant Professor (Guest/Visiting faculty) at the University of Delhi and the Central University of Haryana. \r\nDr. Gautam has received prestigious international travel grants like DST-SERB-International travel support, DBT travel grant, and CSIR Foreign grant for presenting his research work in France, U.S.A, Canada, and Australia. Dr. Gautam has also received prestigious fellowships like DST-SERB-NPDF, UGC-DS Kothari, CSIR-RA, CSIR-JRF, DBT-JRF, and GATE-Biotechnology. Currently, Dr. Gautam is the In-charge of the Biotechnology laboratory and Academic Officer at ICMR-NIIRNCD. \r\n', 'EDUCATIONAL QUALIFICATIONS	\r\n•	Postdoc\r\n        MIGAL - Galilee Research Institute, Israel and Jawaharlal Nehru University, India.\r\n•	Ph.D. -Biotechnology \r\n        Jawaharlal Nehru University, New Delhi, India\r\n•	MSc -Biotechnology\r\n        Jawaharlal Nehru University, New Delhi, India\r\n•	BSc- Biotechnology\r\n        Jamia Millia Islamia, New Delhi, India\r\n\r\n TEACHING EXPERIENCE	\r\n•	Assistant Professor (Guest/Visiting faculty): University of Delhi, India\r\n        Class- M.Sc.\r\n        Subjects taught (Theory and Practical) -\r\n 	- Recombinant DNA Technology and Proteomics (BT-Core-2003)\r\n	- Cell and Molecular Biology (BT-Core-3004)\r\n        - Microbiology (BT-Core-1002)\r\n•	Assistant Professor (Guest/Visiting faculty): \r\n        Department of Biotechnology, Central University of Haryana, India\r\n        Class- M.Sc. Biotechnology\r\n        Subjects taught (Theory and Practical) -\r\n	- Biomolecules and Metabolism (BT_01102CC)\r\n	- Bioinformatics and Biostatistics (BT_01102GEC)\r\n\r\nTRAINING\r\n•	Completed six months of “Executive Program in Public Health Policy, Leadership & Management”-July 2022 batch at AIIMS Jodhpur. \r\n•	Participated in a one-week workshop on “Qualitative Research and Data Analysis” at IIPS Mumbai. \r\n•	Completed one-week “Induction training on Research Methodology, Bio-Statistical techniques, Health Data Management and its analysis, using Statistical Software” at ICMR-NIMS, Delhi.\r\n\r\n', 'Selected Publications in 2025- \r\n1 Sheilina Choudhary, Prince Kumar .... Janesh Kumar Gautam (2025)\r\nMolecular docking of Pyocyanin from Pseudomonas aeruginosa with NADPH oxidase and Toll-like receptor 4: Exploring the Link Between Oral Microbiome and Oral Cancer Pathogenesis.\r\nDOI-10.1101/2025.08.24.671957. \r\n2. Gautam, J.K.⁺, Anand, P.K., Chouhan, M., Babu, B.V. (2025). \r\nCapacity building of Community Health Officers for optimizing the screening and early diagnosis of Oral Cancer in Jodhpur, Rajasthan, India.  \r\nScientific Reports. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-00225-w. Impact Factor: 4.3\r\n3. Gautam, J.K.⁺, Mohanty, S.S., Babu, B.V. (2025). \r\nPrevalence and determinants of Oral Potentially Malignant Disorders in Western Rajasthan. \r\nScientific Reports. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-98096-8. Impact Factor: 4.3\r\n4. Gautam, J.K., Thanvi, A., Soni, P., Anand, P.K. (2025). \r\nEstimation of the Proportion and Determinants of Diabetes Mellitus Among Notified Tuberculosis Patients in Jaipur, Rajasthan, India. \r\nCureus Journal of Medical Sciences. https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.80319. Impact Factor: 1.1\r\n5. Thakor, M., Gautam, J.K., Panigrahi, A., Garasiya, D., Brhamnia, S.L., Mohanty, S.S. (2025). \r\nOperational Feasibility of Point-of-Care Testing for Sickle Cell Disease in Resource-Limited Settings of Tribal Sub-Plan Region of India. \r\nDiagnostics, 15(3), 348. https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics15030348. Impact Factor: 3.1\r\n\r\nSelected Publications in 2024- \r\n6. Anand Nishad, Janesh Kumar Gautam, Ishu Agarwal, Ashis Kumar Nandi. (2024). \r\nImmune Priming Promotes Thermotolerance, Whereas Thermopriming Suppresses Systemic Acquired Resistance in Arabidopsis. \r\nDOI- 10.1111/pce.15364. Impact Factor: 7.6\r\n7. Mittal, D., Gautam, J.K., Varma, M., Laie, A., Mishra, S., Behera, S. Vadassery J. (2024) External jasmonic acid isoleucine mediates amplification PEPR and jasmonate-based immune signalling. \r\nDOI-10.1111/pce.14812. Impact Factor: 7.6\r\n\r\nSelected Publications in 2023- \r\n8. D Singh, V Patil, R Kumar, JK Gautam, V Singh, AK Nandi. (2023).\r\nRSI1/FLD and its epigenetic target RRTF1 are essential for retaining infection memory in Arabidopsis thaliana. \r\nDOI- 10.1111/tpj.16252.Impact Factor: 7.1                                         \r\n\r\nSelected Publications before 2023- \r\n9. Janesh Kumar Gautam, Mrunmay Kumar Giri, Deepjyoti Singh, and Ashis Kumar Nandi (2021). \r\nMYC2 influences salicylic acid biosynthesis and defense against bacterial pathogens in Arabidopsis thaliana. .   \r\nDOI- 10.1111/ppl.13575. Impact Factor: 6.4\r\n10. Vijayata Singh, Deepjyoti Singh, Janesh Kumar Gautam, Nandi, A.K. (2019). \r\nRSI1/FLD is a positive regulator for defense against necrotrophic pathogens.\r\nDOI- 10.1016/j.pmpp.2019.04.005. Impact Factor: 2.7\r\n11.  Janesh Kumar Gautam and Ashis Kumar Nandi (2018).\r\nAPD1, the unique member of the Arabidopsis AP2 family influences systemic acquired resistance and ethylene-jasmonic acid signaling. \r\nDOI-  10.1016/j.plaphy.2018.10.026. Impact Factor: 6.4\r\n12. Mrunmay Kumar Giri1, Janesh Kumar Gautam1, V. prasad, Sudip Chattopadhyay, and Ashis Kumar Nandi (2017) [1= equal contribution]. \r\nMYC2 modulates phenotype, disease defense but not ABA signaling. \r\nDOI- 10.1007/s12038-017-9703-8. Impact Factor: 2.9\r\n13. Bhattacharjee, L, Singh, D, Janesh Kumar Gautam and Nandi, A.K. (2017). \r\n AtSRP4 and AtSRP5 negatively regulate stress-induced cell death and effector- triggered immunity induced by bacterial effector AvrRpt2 \r\nDOI-10.1111/ppl.12516.  Impact Factor: 6.4\r\n14. Giri, M.K., Swain, S., Singh, S., Janesh Kumar Gautam, Singh, N., Bhattacharjee, L., and Nandi, A.K. (2014). \r\nThe Arabidopsis thaliana At4g13040 gene, a unique member of the AP2/EREBP family, is a positive regulator for salicylic acid accumulation and basal defense against bacterial pathogens. \r\nDOI- 10.1016/j.jplph.2013.12.015.  Impact Factor: 4.3\r\n\r\n\r\n', '1. Feasibility of utilization of Community Health Officers in the screening of Oral Cancer - Principal Investigator \r\n2. Assessment of Healthcare workers competence for testing sickle cell disorder in infants and pregnant women using a point-of-care device- Co-Investigator \r\n', '1. Development and validation of CRISPR-Cas integrated Loop-Mediated Isothermal Amplification diagnostic test for the rapid detection of antibiotic- resistant Klebsiella pneumoniae- Principal Investigator\r\n2. Optimizing oral cancer screening through capacity building of Healthcare workers of Health and Wellness Centres of districts of Rajasthan- Principal Investigator\r\n3. Electrochemical Immunobiosensor-Based Point-of-Care for Sickle Cell Disease and β-thalassemia- Co-Investigator\r\n4. Pan India Antigenic characterization of Dengue virus: Early warning signals for potential pandemic- Co-Investigator\r\n5. Implementation Research on Comprehensive Sickle Cell Disease (SCD)  Care under the National Sickle Cell Elimination Mission- - Co-Investigator\r\n\r\n'),
(13, 14, NULL, '\r\nMBBS from Dr. SN Medical College Jodhpur ', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(14, 78, 'Dr. Mukti Khetan is a Scientist-C (Statistics) currently working at ICMR-NIIRNCD in Jodhpur, India. Previously, she held a permanent position as an Assistant Professor in the Department of Statistics at Sambalpur University, a state university located in Odisha. Dr. Khetan completed her M.Sc. in Statistics from Banaras Hindu University Varanasi, and her Ph.D. in Statistics from IIT (ISM) Dhanbad in December 2016. She has 1.5 years of research experience post-PhD from IIT Bombay, in addition to 4 years of teaching experience. She has also worked as Business Analyst at Tata Consultancy Services, Bangalore.  She was appointed as the Economic & Statistical Officer in the Economic and Statistics division of the Uttar Pradesh Public Service Commission, which is under the Government of Uttar Pradesh.\r\nDr. Khetan research interests include reproductive and child health, non-communicable disease, time series analysis, and sampling theory. She has supervised M.Phil. and M.Sc. students. She has published several research papers in reputed international journals using softwares R, Matlab and SPSS. Dr. Khetan has published several book chapters and has served as the editor of a book titled \"Distribution Theory: Principles and Applications\". She has cleared two papers on Actuarial Science and received an award for the best paper presentation.\r\n', '- B.Sc (Statistics & Mathematics - C.S.J.M University, Kanpur, India) \r\n- M.Sc (Statistics - Banaras Hindu University, Varanasi, India) \r\n- Ph.D. (Statistics -IIT Dhanbad, India)\r\n', 'Publications\r\n1.	Khetan, M., & Babu, B. V. (2025). Silicosis prevalence and related issues in India: A scoping review. Journal of Occupational Medicine and Toxicology, 20(3). https://doi.org/10.1186/s12995-024-00445-2. Impact factor: 2.9. Publisher: Springer Nature.\r\n2.	Huda RK, Chowhan RS, Arora S, Khetan M et al. Perceptions of healthcare workers and patients on the implementation of telemedicine in hard-to-reach areas: A qualitative study from rajasthan, India. DIGITAL HEALTH. 2025;11. doi:10.1177/20552076251331874. Impact factor: 2.9.\r\n3.	Sangwan, R. K., Kansara, D., Matoria, S., Ali, H., Khetan, M., Singh, V., Thakor, M., Huda, R. K., & Babu, B. V. (2024). Perceptions of primary healthcare providers for screening and management of mental health disorders in India: A qualitative study. Frontiers in Public Health, 12. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1446606. Impact factor: 3.0. Publisher: Frontiers Media SA.\r\n4.	Arora, S., Huda, R. K., Verma, S., Khetan, M., Sangwan, R. K., Huda, R. K. Sr., & Sangwan, R. K. (2024). Challenges, barriers, and facilitators in telemedicine implementation in India: A scoping review. Cureus, 16(8). Impact factor: 1.2. Publisher: Cureus Inc.\r\n5.	Arshad, M., Khetan, M., Kumar, V., & Pathak, A. K. (2024). Record-based transmuted generalized linear exponential distribution with increasing, decreasing, and bathtub-shaped failure rates. Communications in Statistics - Simulation and Computation, 53(7), 3489-3513. https://doi.org/10.1080/03610918.2024.0000000. Impact factor: 0.8. Publisher: Taylor & Francis.\r\n6.	Pathak, A. K., Arshad, M., Azhad, Q. J., Khetan, M., & Pandey, A. (2023). A novel bivariate generalized Weibull distribution with properties and applications. American Journal of Mathematical and Management Sciences, 42(4), 279-306. Impact factor: 1.8. Publisher: Taylor & Francis.\r\n7.	Arshad, M., Pathak, A. K., Azhad, Q. J., & Khetan, M. (2023). Modeling bivariate data using linear exponential and Weibull distributions as marginals. Mathematica Slovaca, 73(4), 1075-1096. https://doi.org/10.1515/ms-2023-0079. Impact factor: 0.418. Publisher: De Gruyter.\r\n8.	Chesneau, C., Kumar, V., Khetan, M., & Arshad, M. (2022). On a modified weighted exponential distribution with applications. Mathematical and Computational Applications, 27(1), 17. https://doi.org/10.3390/mca27010017. Publisher: MDPI.\r\n9.	Verma, P., Swain, P. K., Singh, K. K., & Khetan, M. (2020). Count data regression modeling: An application to spontaneous abortion. Reproductive Health, 17, 106. Impact factor: 2.836. Publisher: BioMed Central (Springer Nature).\r\n10.	Singh, G. N., Pandey, A. K., & Khetan, M. (2020). Generalized estimation procedure in two-occasion rotation patterns. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, India Section A: Physical Sciences, 92, 195-203. Impact factor: 0.754. Publisher: Springer India.\r\n11.	Singh, G. N., Suman, S., Khetan, M., & Paul, C. (2018). Some estimation procedures of sensitive character using scrambled response techniques in successive sampling. Communications in Statistics - Theory and Methods, 47(8), 1830-1841. Impact factor: 0.612. Publisher: Taylor & Francis.\r\n12.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M., & Maurya, S. (2017). Some estimation procedures under non-response using a super population model in two-occasion successive sampling. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, India Section A: Physical Sciences, 87, 373-384. Impact factor: 0.754. Publisher: Springer.\r\n13.	Singh, G. N., Maurya, S., & Khetan, M. (2017). Some effective combinations of available information under non-response in two-occasion successive sampling. Communications in Statistics - Theory and Methods, 46(1), 280-295. Impact factor: 0.612. Publisher: Taylor & Francis.\r\n14.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M., & Maurya, S. (2017). On the use of multiple auxiliary variables to neutralize the effect of non-response in two-occasion successive sampling. Communications in Statistics - Theory and Methods, 46(7), 3501-3519. Impact factor: 0.612. Publisher: Taylor & Francis.\r\n15.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M., & Maurya, S. (2016). Use of multiple auxiliary variables to cope with non-response in estimation of the current population mean in two-occasion successive sampling. Communications in Statistics - Theory and Methods, 45(16), 4840-4857. Impact factor: 0.612. Publisher: Taylor & Francis.\r\n16.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M., & Maurya, S. (2016). Estimation under non-response when it occurs on both occasions in two-occasion successive sampling. Communications in Statistics - Theory and Methods, 45(13), 3939-3951. Impact factor: 0.612. Publisher: Taylor & Francis.\r\n17.	Singh, G. N., Maurya, S., Khetan, M., & Kadilar, C. (2016). Some imputation methods for missing data in sample surveys. Hacettepe Journal of Mathematics and Statistics, 45(6), 1865-1880. Impact factor: 0.98. Publisher: University of Hacettepe.\r\n18.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M., & Maurya, S. (2015). Assessment of exponential methods of estimation under non-response in two-occasion successive sampling. Journal of Statistical Theory and Practice, 9, 506-523. Publisher: Taylor & Francis.\r\n19.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M. and Maurya, S. (2014): Some efficient estimation procedures under non- response in two-occasion successive sampling. Statistica, Vol. anno LXXIV. No. 3, pp. 295-313.\r\n20.	Singh, G. N., Maurya, S. and Khetan, M. (2014): Estimation of population mean under multiple imputation in sample surveys. International Journal Mathematics and Statistics. Vol. 15, No. 3, pp. 36-46.\r\n21.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M. and Maurya, S. (2015): Exponential Methods of Estimation under Non-Response in Two-Occasion Rotation Patterns, International Journal of Statistics and Economics, Vol. 16, No. 1, pp. 8-19. ISSN 0975-556X.\r\n22.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M. and Maurya, S. (2015): General Estimation Procedures With Multi Auxiliary Variables Under Non-Response In Two-Occasion Successive Sampling. International Journal of Statistics and Economics, Vol. 16, No. 2, pp. 87-107. ISSN 0975-556X.\r\n23.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M. and Maurya, S. (2015): Some Estimation Procedures Under Non-Response In Two-Occasion Successive Sampling. International Journal of Mathematics & Statistics, Vol. 16, No. 1, pp. 25-44. ISSN 0974-7117.\r\n24.	Singh, G. N., Maurya, S. and Khetan, M. (2015): An efficient class of estimators of population mean in two-phase sampling, International Journal of Statistics &Economics, Vol. 16, No. 1, pp. 69-82. ISSN 0975-556X.\r\n25.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M. and Maurya, S (2016): Some Effective Estimation Procedures under Non-Response in Two-Phase Successive Sampling, Statistics in Transition New Series, Vol. 17, No. 2, pp. 163-182.\r\n26.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M. and Maurya, S. (2016): Exponential Methods of Imputation in Estimation of Current Population Mean in Two-Occasion Successive Sampling. International Journal of Mathematics & Statistics, Vol. 17, No. 2, pp. 12-35. ISSN 0974-7117.\r\n27.	Singh, G. N., Khetan, M. and Maurya, S. (2016): A generalized estimation procedure on two successive occasions in cluster sampling. Journal of Applied Probability and Statistics, Vol. 2, No. 2, pp. 83-93.\r\n28.	Singh, G. N., Suman. S. and Khetan, M. (2016): Almost unbiased estimation procedure of finite population mean under non-response in sample surveys. International Journal of Mathematics and Computation, Vo. 28, No.1, pp. 33-47.\r\n29.	Singh, G. N., Suman. S. and Khetan, M. (2016): Estimation of population mean under scramble response in sample surveys. Journal of the Indian Society of Agriculture Statistics, Vol. 70, No. 2, pp. 97-110.\r\n30.	Khetan, M., Singh, G. N. and Verma, P. (2018): A linear estimation Procedures using super population model in two-occasion successive sampling under non-response. International Journal of Mathematics & Statistics, Vol 19, No.1, pp. 41-54. ISSN 0974-7117.\r\n31.	Khetan, M., Singh, G. N. and Verma, P. (2018): Generalized exponential methods of imputation in estimation of current population mean in two-occasion successive sampling. International Journal of Statistics &Economics Vol 19, No. 1, pp. 38-47. ISSN 0975-556X.\r\n32.	Khetan Mukti, Savita Pareek, Siuli Mukhopadhyay, and Kalyan Das (2021): Clustering gene expression time series data embedded in a nonparametric setup. Journal of Statistical Research 55, no. 1, 207-224.\r\n33.	Khetan, M., Kumar, V., Pradhan, K. C., Shaikh, M. A., & Arshad, M. (2024). Forecasting gold price and inflation for India and US: An analysis of ARIMA and Holt Winters models. Theoretical and Applied Economics, 31(2(639)), 255-268.\r\n\r\nBook Published:\r\n1.	Published one edited Book, “Distribution Theory: Principles and Applications”, in September 2023 in CRC Press (Taylor & Fransis).\r\n\r\nChapter Published:\r\n2.	Vijay Kumar, Mukti Khetan, Mohd. Arshad, and Shweta Dixit (2023). Random Variables and Their Properties. Book Chapter, AAP, co-publishing with CRC Press, Taylor & Francis. Hard ISBN: 9781774912140 E-Book ISBN: 9781003336303.\r\n3.	Mukti Khetan, Vijay Kumar, Mohd. Arshad, and Prashant Verma (2023). Discrete Distributions. Book Chapter, AAP, co-publishing with CRC Press, Taylor & Francis. Hard ISBN: 9781774912140 E-Book ISBN: 9781003336303.\r\n4.	Shweta Dixit, Mukti Khetan, Mohd. Arshad, Prashant Verma, and Ashok Kumar Pathak (2023). Continuous Distributions. Book Chapter, AAP, co-publishing with CRC Press, Taylor & Francis. Hard ISBN: 9781774912140 E-Book ISBN: 9781003336303.\r\n5.	Mohd. Arshad, Vijay Kumar, Mukti Khetan, and Fozia Homa (2023). Family of Weibull Distributions. Book Chapter, AAP, co-publishing with CRC Press, Taylor & Francis. Hard ISBN: 9781774912140 E-Book ISBN: 9781003336303.\r\n6.	Mohd Arshad, Mukti Khetan, Vijay Kumar, and Fozia Homa (2023). Life Distributions. Book Chapter, AAP, co-publishing with CRC Press, Taylor & Francis. Hard ISBN: 9781774912140 E-Book ISBN: 9781003336303.\r\n7.	Prashant Verma, Shweta Dixit, Mukti Khetan, Suresh Badarla, and Fozia Homa (2023). Dynamics of Data Analysis and Visualization with Python. Book Chapter, AAP, co-publishing with CRC Press, Taylor & Francis. Hard ISBN: 9781774912140 E-Book ISBN: 9781003336303.\r\n8.	Pathak, Ashok Kumar, Mohd Arshad, Sanjeev Bakshi, Mukti Khetan, and Sherry Mangla (2023): A New Alpha Power Transformed Weibull Distribution. G Families of Probability Distributions: Theory and Practices 302. eBook ISBN9781003232193.\r\n9.	Homa, Fozia, Mukti Khetan, and Shweta Dixit (2019). Assessment of Sampling Techniques: Theory and Application. Book Chapter, AAP, co-publishing with CRC Press, Taylor & Francis. ISBN: 9781771887526.\r\n10.	Khetan Mukti, Prashant Verma, Shweta Dixit, and F. Homa (2019). The Axioms of Statistical Inference: Estimation and Hypothesis Testing. Book Chapter, AAP, co-publishing with CRC Press, Taylor & Francis. ISBN: 9781771887526.\r\n', '1. Spatial Epidemiological Approach to Malaria Elimination Research (SEAMER)	', 'As PI\r\n1.	High population-based coverage of high-risk pregnancy identification and management through effective antenatal care and care continuum across antenatal, intrapartum and postnatal care- An Implementation Research. \r\n2.	Implementation research on primordial prevention of NCDs among school-going children. \r\n3.     Implementation Research on Enhancing Trauma Care at Health and Wellness Centres\r\n\r\n\r\nAs Co-PI\r\n1.     Optimizing Adolescent Healthcare Services Through Health and Wellness Centres in Sikar District of Rajasthan: An Implementation Research: Principal Investigator\r\n2.	Capacity Building Training for Frontline Health Workers for Screening and Management of Common Mental Health Disorders in Primary Care: Principal Investigator.\r\n3.	Acceptance of digital medical tools and telemedicine in hard-to-reach areas as augmentation of conventional physical clinics and face-to-face consultation: An interventional study: Co-PI.\r\n4.	An Implementation Research to strengthen DIGItal technologies for optimizing the Continuum of CARE for Diabetes and Hypertension Management in the Public Healthcare System in India (DIGI-CARE): Co-PI\r\n');
